  { id:'opqb-t5-165', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Distichiasis** is:',
    options:['Misdirected eyelashes','Accessory row of eyelashes','Downward drooping of upper lid','Outward protrusion of lower lid'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Distichiasis is a SECOND row of lashes growing where the meibomian gland orifices should be** — on the posterior lid margin, behind the normal anterior lash row. The extra lashes are usually fine, short and pale, and because they emerge from the posterior margin they point straight at the globe.\n\n⚠️ **This is a gap in the course material, and it was measured rather than assumed.** Every file in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and `content\\ophtho\\book\\` was grepped case-insensitively for `distichi`, and there are exactly three hits — every one of them an *absence register*, not teaching. `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5 lists distichiasis among the lid-margin terms \"ABSENT\" from printed 70–81; `L5) Eyelid disease` lists it under \"conditions absent altogether\", zero hits in its typed text and zero in its slide blocks; and `L27,28) Problems of External Appearance` uses the word once, inside a `[my reading, not printed]` note on an unlabelled photograph. **No lecture and no book chapter defines it.** The definition above and the mechanism below are therefore *not taken from the course material*.\n\n**Why the distinction matters clinically.** All three of the following put lashes against the cornea, and the treatment differs:\n\n| Term | Lid margin | Lashes | Where they arise |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Trichiasis** | **Normal** | Misdirected, turned back | The normal anterior row |\n| **Entropion** | **Rotated inward** | Normally sited, carried in with the margin | The normal anterior row |\n| **Distichiasis** | Normal | Extra row, aimed at the globe | The **meibomian orifices**, posterior margin |\n\nThe book prints the first two of those rows itself. Printed 78: \"The eyelid margin is in its normal anatomic condition, but the eyelashes are misdirected toward the eye\" — trichiasis. Printed 77: \"The eyelid margin is rotated inwards toward the eye\" — entropion. Distichiasis is the third case the book never reaches.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and each of them is a term this same topic keys elsewhere:**\n\n| Option | What it actually names | Where this bank keys it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Misdirected eyelashes** | **Trichiasis** | `opqb-t5-208` keys the trichiasis/entropion discriminator, and `opqb-t5-223` is a trichiasis vignette |\n| **Downward drooping of upper lid** | **Ptosis** — book printed 75, \"Drooping of the upper eyelid below its normal position, so that it covers more than 2 mm of the superior limbus\" | the ptosis chain runs `opqb-t5-169`, `-172`, `-174`, `-181`, `-187`, `-194`, `-217` |\n| **Outward protrusion of lower lid** | **Ectropion** — book printed 77, \"rotated outward away from the eye … It mainly affects the lower lid\" | `opqb-t5-185` |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** If lashes are rubbing, count the rows and look at the margin. One row from a normal margin = trichiasis. One row carried in by an inturned margin = entropion. **Two rows, the extra one coming out of the meibomian line = distichiasis** — and that one is usually congenital and needs the aberrant follicles destroyed, not the margin rotated *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Distichiasis is an accessory posterior row of lashes arising from the meibomian orifices; trichiasis is misdirection of the normal row and entropion is inturning of the whole margin.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.25 (Q165), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-166', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A patient complains of **maceration of skin of the lids** and **conjunctival redness at the inner and outer canthi**. Conjunctival swab is expected to show:',
    options:['Staphylococcus aureus.','Streptococcus viridans.','Streptococcus pneumoniae','Morax–Axenfeld diplobacilli'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Two words in the stem give it away: *canthi* and *maceration*.** Disease confined to the inner and outer angles, with the skin there sodden, white and fissured rather than crusted, is **angular blepharoconjunctivitis**, and its organism is the **Morax–Axenfeld diplobacillus** (*Moraxella lacunata*).\n\n**The mechanism explains the site.** *Moraxella* elaborates a **proteolytic enzyme** that digests the keratin of the skin at the lid angles. Macerated, excoriated skin at the canthi with a scanty mucoid discharge and no ulceration of the lash line is the picture; the rest of the lid margin, and the lashes, are spared. That is the opposite of staphylococcal blepharitis, which sits **along the whole lash line** and ulcerates it.\n\n⚠️ **Gap, and it was measured.** Grepping every file in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and `content\\ophtho\\book\\` for `Morax`: the hits in `L6) Conjunctiva` and in `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.6 are both **absence registers** — L6's organism audit records \"Morax / Moraxella = 0\" and notes that its \"'Angular' conjunctivitis is titled without its organism\", and the book chapter lists \"angular conjunctivitis (Moraxella lacunata): absent\". The one live hit in the whole module is `L15,16) Cornea`, which names **Moraxella** among the organisms of bacterial keratitis in \"elderly, debilitated patients\" — a real hit, opened, but it is keratitis, not angular blepharitis. **So the organism is in the module; this disease is not.** The clinical picture above is *not taken from the course material*.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it really causes on the lid or conjunctiva |\n|---|---|\n| **Staphylococcus aureus** | The bank's default lid organism, and correct for the two hordeola and for ulcerative blepharitis — book printed 72 gives it to both hordeolum externum and internum, printed 74 to \"Staphylococcal (ulcerative)\" blepharitis. But staphylococcal disease takes the **whole lash line** with hard collarettes and margin ulceration, not the canthi with macerated skin. |\n| **Streptococcus viridans** | An oral commensal. It is not a lid pathogen at all; it appears in ophthalmology mainly as a cause of **delayed, low-grade post-operative endophthalmitis**, which is a different chapter entirely. Printed \"viridians\" in the source. |\n| **Streptococcus pneumoniae** | A genuine cause of acute purulent **conjunctivitis**, especially in children, but it gives a diffusely red eye with mucopurulent discharge — not a canthus-limited disease with macerated skin. Printed \"pneumonia\" in the source, i.e. the disease name for the organism name. |\n\n**Note on the printing.** The source spells option B \"Streptococcus viridians.\" and option C \"Streptococcus pneumonia\"; both are repaired above and the printings are recorded here.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Blepharitis that respects the angles and macerates the skin there is *Moraxella* until proved otherwise, and it responds to topical **zinc** preparations or tetracycline, which inhibit the proteolytic enzyme *(not taken from the course material)*. This bank asks the same organism again as bare recall at `opqb-t5-175`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Angular blepharoconjunctivitis — maceration of the skin at the inner and outer canthi — is caused by the Morax–Axenfeld diplobacillus (Moraxella lacunata), not by the staphylococci and streptococci that cause lash-line and diffuse conjunctival disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.25 (Q166), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-167', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Chalazion** is a chronic inflammatory granuloma of:',
    options:['Meibomian Gland',"Zeis's gland",'Sweat gland',"Wolfring's gland"],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Chalazion (Meibomian cyst): Sterile chronic granulomatous inflammatory lesion of the meibomian glands due to retained sebaceous secretions**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The book prints the same definition almost word for word.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 73: \"Sterile chronic granulomatous inflammatory lesion of the meibomian glands caused by retained sebaceous secretions\", and under Incidence, \"The most common benign non-neoplastic lesion of the eyelids\" — which is why `opqb-t5-177` keys chalazion as the commonest painless lid swelling.\n\n**Every word of that definition is doing work.**\n\n| Word | Why it is there |\n|---|---|\n| **Sterile** | There is no organism. It is an obstructed gland, not an infection — which is why antibiotics do nothing for an uncomplicated chalazion and why the treatment is warm compresses, intralesional steroid, or incision and curettage. When it *does* become infected it stops being a chalazion and becomes a hordeolum internum, the situation of `opqb-t5-215`. |\n| **Chronic** | Weeks to months, gradually enlarging. This is the discriminator against the hordeolum, and `opqb-t5-211` keys exactly it. |\n| **Granulomatous** | Retained lipid leaks into the tarsus and provokes a lipogranuloma — the histology behind the firm, rubbery feel. |\n| **Meibomian glands** | Modified sebaceous glands **inside the tarsal plate**, opening on the posterior lid margin. That is why the book's sign is \"A firm nodule **within the tarsal plate**\", why the lesion sits away from the lash line, and why it points on the **conjunctival** side. |\n\n**Why the other three glands are wrong — and all three are real lid glands, which is the trap:**\n\n| Option | The gland | What it actually causes |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Zeis's gland** | A **sebaceous** gland attached to a lash follicle, in the anterior lamella | Acute infection of it is **hordeolum externum — the stye**, which is what `opqb-t5-212` keys. Book printed 72: \"the follicle of the lash or the associated gland of Zeiss\". |\n| **Sweat gland** | The lid's modified apocrine sweat gland is the **gland of Moll**, also at the lash root | Blockage gives a clear cyst of Moll (hidrocystoma); infection contributes to the stye. Not a granuloma of the tarsus. |\n| **Wolfring's gland** | An **accessory lacrimal gland** at the upper border of the tarsus | It secretes tears, not lipid. The book prints it only as a figure label on printed 71, where the cache notes it is \"the ONLY accessory lacrimal gland eponym printed anywhere in chapters 4 or 5\". It has no chalazion. |\n\n**Note on the printing.** The source sets these as \"Zies's gland\" and \"Wolfing's gland\"; both are repaired above.",
    objective:'A chalazion is a sterile chronic lipogranuloma of a meibomian gland within the tarsal plate — not of Zeis (stye), Moll (sweat) or Wolfring (accessory lacrimal).',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.25 (Q167), key p.31 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-168', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:"Ptosis in **Horner's syndrome** is due to paralysis of:",
    options:["Riolan's muscle","Horner's muscle","Muller's muscle",'The levator palpebrae muscle','Orbicularis oculi muscle'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The book answers this in one parenthesis.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 76, aetiology 4 of ptosis: \"**Sympathetic:** As in Horner's syndrome **(affecting Muller's muscle)**.\"\n\n**Why Müller's muscle and not the levator.** Two muscles lift the upper lid, and they have different nerve supplies:\n\n| Muscle | Supply | Contribution to lid height |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Levator palpebrae superioris** | Oculomotor (III), somatic | The bulk of it — 15 mm of excursion |\n| **Müller's muscle** (superior tarsal) | **Sympathetic**, from the superior cervical ganglion | About **2 mm** of tonic lift |\n\nA sympathetic lesion anywhere along the three-neurone chain therefore takes away only that 2 mm. The result is the characteristic Horner ptosis: **partial, mild, and never covering the pupil**, with the palpebral fissure narrowed from **both** ends because the lower lid's own sympathetically innervated retractor relaxes too — the \"upside-down ptosis\". `L5) Eyelid disease` slide 11 is headed \"Ptosis / Sympathetic paralysis (Horner Syndrome)\" and photographs precisely that: a child whose right upper lid sits slightly lower, right fissure narrowed, lower lid slightly elevated, **right pupil smaller**, and the right iris paler than the left — the heterochromia of congenital Horner. The deck prints no words on that slide; the picture is the teaching.\n\n**Why the other four are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it is | Effect if paralysed |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Riolan's muscle** | The slip of orbicularis running along the lid margin behind the lash roots. `L5` slide 2's anatomy diagram prints it as \"Muscle of Riolan\" | Keeps the margin apposed to the globe. Nothing to do with lid height |\n| **Horner's muscle** | The lacrimal part of orbicularis, behind the lacrimal sac — the eponym is a **different Horner**, and it is a lacrimal pump, not a lid elevator | Failure impairs tear drainage, causing epiphora — a `op-lac` problem |\n| **The levator palpebrae muscle** | The main elevator, III nerve | Paralysis gives **complete** ptosis with the eye down-and-out and the pupil dilated — the picture of `opqb-t5-170`, not of Horner |\n| **Orbicularis oculi muscle** | The lid **sphincter**, VII nerve | Paralysis causes failure of **closure** — lagophthalmos, ectropion — the exact opposite malposition, keyed at `opqb-t5-201` and `-206` |\n\n**Note on the printing.** The source spells option A \"Rolan's muscle\", repaired above to Riolan after the label on `L5` slide 2; and option D \"The levator **palpebral** muscle\" for *palpebrae*. Option C is left exactly as printed, \"Muller's muscle\" without the umlaut — which is also how the book sets it on printed 76, while the book's own figure on printed 71 labels it \"Müller muscle\". ⚠️ **This is the only five-option question (A–E) in the topic.**\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Mild ptosis **plus a small pupil** is sympathetic; complete ptosis **plus a large pupil** is the third nerve. The two are asked back to back here and at `opqb-t5-170`, and the corpus works the localisation of the sympathetic lesion at `opmcq-c1-4`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:"Horner's ptosis is loss of the ~2 mm sympathetic lift of Müller's muscle, giving a partial ptosis with miosis and a narrowed fissure; the levator is III-supplied and orbicularis is VII-supplied.",
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.25 (Q168), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-169', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Severe congenital ptosis with no levator function** can be treated by:',
    options:['Levator resection from skin side','Levator resection from conjunctival side','Fascia lata sling operation','Fasanella–Servat operation'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The operation is chosen by the levator function, and the lecture prints the numbers.** `L5) Eyelid disease` has body text on exactly two of its 47 slides, and they are the two that decide this:\n\n- **Slide 8, \"Levator resection\":** \"Indicated for any ptosis provided levator function is **at least 5 mm**.\"\n- **Slide 9, \"Frontalis brow suspension\":** main indications — \"**Severe ptosis with poor levator function ( 4 mm or less )**\" and \"Marcus Gunn jaw-winking syndrome\". Its printed caption: \"Attachment of tarsus to frontalis muscle with sling\".\n\nThe book prints the same rule, but only inside a figure. `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 76 carries a two-panel surgical plate whose headers read \"**Levator resection / Good levator function**\" and \"**Frontalis sling / Poor levator function**\"; the body text says merely \"Surgical (levator resection, frontalis sling, etc)\". The selection rule exists in that figure and nowhere else in the chapter.\n\n**Why absent levator function forces the sling.** A levator resection *shortens a muscle that works*. If the levator has no excursion at all, shortening it moves the lid up in primary position and then leaves it there — it cannot follow the eye, and lagophthalmos on downgaze and on sleep is worse than the ptosis was. A **frontalis sling** does not use the levator at all: a strip of **fascia lata** (or a synthetic equivalent) is passed subcutaneously from the tarsus up over the brow and anchored to frontalis, so the patient lifts the lid with the eyebrow. That is why the answer names the *material* — fascia lata — rather than the eponym.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it is | Why not here |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Levator resection from skin side** | The standard anterior approach of slide 8's drawings | Needs ≥ 5 mm of levator function. There is none |\n| **Levator resection from conjunctival side** | The posterior approach to the same muscle | Same objection, and the posterior route is used for **small** ptoses with good function, the opposite end of the range |\n| **Fasanella–Servat operation** | Excision of upper tarsal border, conjunctiva and Müller's muscle through the conjunctival side | A **1–2 mm** operation for **minimal** ptosis with **good** function — classically the Horner lid. Useless for a severe ptosis with none. ⚠️ Both the book (printed 70–81) and `L5` record this operation as absent; the description is *not taken from the course material* |\n\n**Note on the printing.** The source sets option D as \"Fasanella servant operation\" and option C as \"Fascia Lata sling operation\"; the eponym is repaired above.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Measure levator excursion first — `opqb-t5-217` keys that as the single most important examination — then apply the rule: **≥ 5 mm → resect the levator; ≤ 4 mm → sling to frontalis**. The same rule is asked from the other side at `opqb-t5-174` and as a management statement at `opqb-t5-181`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Ptosis surgery is selected by levator function: ≥5 mm allows levator resection, ≤4 mm (or none) requires a frontalis sling, classically of fascia lata.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.25 (Q169), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-170', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Ptosis and mydriasis** are seen in:',
    options:['Facial palsy','Peripheral neuritis','Oculomotor palsy','Sympathetic palsy'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Only one nerve carries both the main lid elevator and the pupil constrictor, and that is the third.** The oculomotor nerve supplies levator palpebrae superioris, four of the six extraocular muscles, and — through its parasympathetic fibres — the sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle. A complete palsy therefore gives all three signs at once: **complete ptosis**, the eye **down and out**, and a **dilated, unreactive pupil**.\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` slide 10 is headed \"Ptosis: Oculomotor paralysis\" and photographs it in two frames: an elderly patient with the left upper lid completely shut, then the same lid held up by the examiner's fingers to reveal the eye **deviated out and down with a visibly dilated pupil**. The deck prints no words — the pair of photographs is the teaching. The book lists the same aetiology as ptosis type 2, printed 76: \"**Paralytic:** Due to III nerve (oculomotor nerve) palsy.\"\n\n**Why option D is the trap, and it is the whole question.** A **sympathetic** palsy also produces ptosis — that is Horner's syndrome, keyed two questions earlier at `opqb-t5-168`. But the sympathetic supply to the eye runs to **Müller's muscle** and to the **dilator** pupillae. Lose it and the pupil goes **small**, not large. So the pairing separates them cleanly:\n\n| | Oculomotor palsy | Sympathetic palsy (Horner) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ptosis | **Complete** — levator paralysed | **Partial**, ~2 mm — only Müller's |\n| Pupil | **Mydriasis**, poorly reactive | **Miosis**, reactive |\n| Eye position | **Down and out** | Normal |\n| Fissure | Closed | Narrowed from **both** lids |\n\n**Why the other two are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it does to the lid |\n|---|---|\n| **Facial palsy** | VII supplies **orbicularis oculi**, the closer. Its palsy gives the opposite malposition — the lid will not **close** (lagophthalmos, paralytic ectropion), which is the whole of `opqb-t5-201`, `-202` and `-216`. It never causes ptosis and never touches the pupil |\n| **Peripheral neuritis** | A non-specific term for peripheral nerve inflammation. It names no particular nerve and produces no defined lid-and-pupil combination. Included as a discard |\n\n**Clinical bottom line — and this is the one to carry out of the topic.** A **painful** third-nerve palsy **with a dilated pupil**, of sudden onset, is a **posterior communicating artery aneurysm** until imaging says otherwise, because the pupillomotor fibres run superficially in the nerve and are compressed first. A pupil-**sparing** third-nerve palsy in a diabetic or hypertensive patient is far more often microvascular *(this triage rule is not taken from the course material — `L5` prints no words on slide 10, and the book gives the aetiology in one line)*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Ptosis with mydriasis is a third-nerve palsy (levator plus pupil sphincter); sympathetic palsy gives partial ptosis with miosis, and facial palsy causes failure of closure, not droop.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.25 (Q170), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-171', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'The **lid sphincter muscle** has the following portions:',
    options:['Palpebral and orbital portions',"Horner's muscle and Riolan's muscle",'All of the above','None of the above'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The lid sphincter is the orbicularis oculi, and its primary division is orbital versus palpebral.** The book names the muscle and its job on printed 71: \"Muscular layer containing fibers of the **orbicularis oculi**, the muscle responsible for **blinking and forcible closure** of the eyelids. It is supplied by the **facial (VII) nerve**.\" That single clause is the whole of the chapter's prose on it.\n\n**The two portions, and why the split is functional rather than descriptive:**\n\n| Portion | Where it lies | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Palpebral** | Within the lids themselves, in front of the septum and tarsus | **Involuntary blinking** and gentle closure — the part that works all day |\n| **Orbital** | A ring outside the orbital rim, over the brow and cheek | **Forcible, voluntary** closure — screwing the eyes shut |\n\nThe book's own anatomy figure on printed 71 subdivides the palpebral part further, labelling \"Orbicularis oculi m. (**preseptal** portion)\" and \"Orbicularis oculi m. (**pretarsal** portion)\" beside the \"(**orbital** portion)\" — a three-way division that the cache flags as figure-only teaching, stated nowhere in the words.\n\n⚠️ **Why option B is a real temptation and the key is still A.** Both structures named in B *are* parts of orbicularis oculi. **Horner's muscle** is its lacrimal part, hooking behind the lacrimal sac to work the tear pump; the **muscle of Riolan** is the marginal slip running behind the lash roots, and `L5) Eyelid disease` slide 2 prints it as a label — \"Muscle of Riolan\" — on its sagittal diagram. So a candidate who knows those two eponyms will reach for \"All of the above\". **The bank keys A**, and the key is recorded as printed, not disputed. The defensible reading is that A names the muscle's **portions** — the standard anatomical division — while Horner's and Riolan's are named *slips* of one of those portions, not portions in their own right; and neither eponym is printed in any book chapter of this module.\n\n**Why C and D are wrong:** C fails because B does not name the portions; D fails because A is correct.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The orbicularis is the **closer** and the levator/Müller pair are the **openers**, and they have different nerves — VII versus III and sympathetic. That is the fact that separates ptosis from lagophthalmos throughout this topic, and it is why a VII palsy gives the picture of `opqb-t5-201` while a III palsy gives that of `opqb-t5-170`.\n\n**Note on the printing.** The source sets option B as \"Horner's muscle and Rolan's muscle\"; Riolan is restored above after the label on `L5` slide 2.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Orbicularis oculi, the lid sphincter, divides into orbital (forcible closure) and palpebral (blinking) portions and is supplied by the facial nerve.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.25 (Q171), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-172', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Congenital ptosis** is characterized by:',
    options:['Absent eyelid crease','Chin elevation','Forehead corrugation','All of the above'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**All three follow from one lesion: the levator muscle never developed properly, so it neither lifts nor creases the lid, and the child compensates.** The book gives the lesion on printed 76, aetiology 1: \"Due to **lack of development of levator muscle**, resulting in a **smooth lid with loss of tarsal fold** [poor levator function]\".\n\n| Sign | Why it happens |\n|---|---|\n| **Absent eyelid crease** | The crease is made by the levator **aponeurosis inserting into the skin** through orbicularis. A dysplastic levator makes no such insertion, so the lid is smooth and the tarsal fold is lost. The book states this one outright |\n| **Chin elevation** | If the lid covers the pupil the child tips the **head back** to see under it — an abnormal head posture adopted for the same reason a squinting child adopts one |\n| **Forehead corrugation** | The child recruits **frontalis** to lift the brow and drag the lid up with it. Sustained brow elevation wrinkles the forehead. This is also why frontalis is the muscle a sling borrows — see `opqb-t5-169` |\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` slide 6 is headed \"Ptosis / Congenital\" and carries four photographs with no words at all. Its second picture shows \"the affected upper lid low and the **lid crease absent/poorly formed**\", and its fourth shows \"bilateral droop with the **brows elevated by frontalis overaction**\" — two of the three signs photographed, neither named. The deck prints no definition, no severity grading and no amblyopia note anywhere on that slide.\n\n**Why \"All of the above\" is forced rather than lazy.** Each of the first three is independently a textbook feature, and the stem is inclusive (\"is characterized by\"), not comparative. Contrast `opqb-t5-187`, where the same disease is put as a single-best question and only one statement survives.\n\n**Two features the options do not mention, and both are worth having.** First, **lid lag on downgaze**: the fibrotic congenital levator cannot relax, so the affected lid sits *higher* than its fellow when the child looks down — the reverse of an acquired ptosis, and the single most useful bedside discriminator. Second, **amblyopia**: a lid across the visual axis deprives a developing eye, which is why severe cases are operated early and is exactly what `opqb-t5-187` keys. *(Both are supplied here — `L5` names amblyopia nowhere in 47 slides, measured on the deck's own typed text, and the book's ptosis section prints no consequence either.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Congenital ptosis shows an absent lid crease from levator maldevelopment, with chin elevation and forehead corrugation as the compensations.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.25 (Q172), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-173', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Inflammation of lid glands** includes all of the following **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Ulcerative blepharitis','Hordeolum externum','Hordeolum Internum','Squamous blepharitis'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Three of these are infections of a gland; the fourth is a scaling disorder of the skin of the lid margin.**\n\n| Option | Structure inflamed | Sourced at |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ulcerative blepharitis** | The **lash follicles and their associated glands of Zeis**, staphylococcal, ulcerating the margin | Book printed 74, \"b. Staphylococcal (ulcerative)\" |\n| **Hordeolum externum** | \"the follicle of the lash or the associated **gland of Zeiss**\" | Book printed 72, hordeolum table |\n| **Hordeolum internum** | \"Acute inflammation of **Meibomian glands**\" | Book printed 72, hordeolum table |\n| **Squamous blepharitis** | **No gland.** Dry or greasy **scaling of the lid-margin skin** | — |\n\n**\"Squamous\" is the older name for the seborrhoeic type**, and the book teaches it as \"a. **Seborrheic**\" on printed 73: \"Associated with generalised seborrheic dermatitis … Hyperaemic lid margin … the presence of **greasy soft scales on the lashes. (no ulceration)**\". The pathology is a disorder of the *skin* — an extension of seborrhoeic dermatitis of the scalp and brows, which is why the book's treatment is lid hygiene **plus treatment of the associated scalp seborrhoea**, and why the vignette at `opqb-t5-220` diagnoses it from dandruff on the eyebrows and a greasy face rather than from anything happening in a gland.\n\n**The distinction the question is really drilling.** Anterior blepharitis has two forms and they behave differently:\n\n| | Seborrhoeic (squamous) | Staphylococcal (ulcerative) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Scales | **Soft, greasy**, sleeving the lash shafts | **Hard, dry collarettes** round the lash bases |\n| Margin | Hyperaemic, **no ulceration** | **Ulcerated**, notched, scarred |\n| Lashes | Intact | Fall out — madarosis, trichiasis, poliosis |\n| Gland involved | None | Zeis / lash follicle |\n\nThe book's cache records that this contrast is visible only by comparing the photographs on printed 73 and 74 — neither page states it in words. `L5` slides 22 and 23 photograph the same contrast, again with no text: slide 22 shows \"hard, brittle, YELLOW-WHITE **COLLARETTES**\" and a \"raw lid margin\" with \"broken and distorted lashes\"; slide 23 shows \"soft, greasy **WHITE SCALES** … wrapped round the lash SHAFTS\" with the margin \"intact, NOT ulcerated\".\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** If the margin is *ulcerated*, a gland is infected and the lashes are at risk — the complications of `opqb-t5-178` and `opqb-t5-204` follow. If it is merely *scaly and greasy*, treat the skin, and look at the scalp.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Ulcerative blepharitis and both hordeola inflame lid glands; squamous (seborrhoeic) blepharitis is a scaling disorder of the lid-margin skin with no glandular infection.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.25 (Q173), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-174', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Levator resection** operation should be used in:',
    options:['Mild ptosis with levator function','Moderate ptosis with good levator function','Moderate ptosis with poor levator function','Severe ptosis with poor levator function'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**This is `opqb-t5-169` from the other side, and the same two lecture slides settle it.** `L5) Eyelid disease` slide 8, one of only two slides in the whole 47 with body text: \"**Levator resection — Indicated for any ptosis provided levator function is at least 5 mm.**\" Its printed captions add the dosing rule: \"Shortening of levator complex\" and \"**Amount determined by levator function and severity of ptosis**\". Slide 9 gives the other half: frontalis brow suspension for \"Severe ptosis with poor levator function ( **4 mm or less** )\".\n\nThe book prints the identical rule in the headers of its surgical plate, printed 76 — \"**Levator resection / Good levator function**\" against \"**Frontalis sling / Poor levator function**\" — and the cache flags that plate as \"the chapter's single most important figure-only item\", because the body text says only \"Surgical (levator resection, frontalis sling, etc)\".\n\n**Why the operation needs a working muscle.** A resection shortens the levator complex so that its existing excursion starts from a higher position. The excursion itself is not increased — it is *repositioned*. A muscle with good function therefore ends up with the lid higher in primary position **and still able to follow the eye down**. A muscle with poor function that is shortened gives a lid stuck high: lagophthalmos, exposure, and a worse eye than before.\n\n**Why each option succeeds or fails:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Mild ptosis with levator function** | The source leaves this one incomplete — it never says *how much* function, and \"mild\" ptosis with good function is classically handled by a smaller posterior-approach procedure rather than a full levator resection. Not the best answer |\n| **Moderate ptosis with good levator function** | ✅ Exactly slide 8's indication: function ≥ 5 mm, and enough ptosis to be worth operating |\n| **Moderate ptosis with poor levator function** | Fails the ≥ 5 mm rule. Poor function goes to a sling whatever the degree |\n| **Severe ptosis with poor levator function** | Slide 9's own wording, verbatim — and it is the **frontalis sling** indication, which is what `opqb-t5-169` keys |\n\n**Note on the printing.** The source sets \"good **levato** function\" and \"poor **levato** function\" in options B and C — the same typo twice — and leaves option A without its adjective. Both are repaired above.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** One number decides the operation, and `opqb-t5-217` keys measuring it as the most important examination in a congenital ptosis: **≥ 5 mm → levator resection · ≤ 4 mm → frontalis sling.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Levator resection is indicated when levator function is at least 5 mm; below that a frontalis sling is required, whatever the degree of ptosis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.25 (Q174), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-175', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Angular blepharitis** is an infection caused by:',
    options:['Staphylococcus aureus',"Koch–Weeks bacillus",'Morax–Axenfeld diplobacillus','Pneumococci'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Angular disease is *Moraxella* disease.** The Morax–Axenfeld diplobacillus (*Moraxella lacunata*) produces a **proteolytic enzyme** that macerates the keratinised skin at the **inner and outer canthi**, giving the sodden, white, fissured angles with scanty mucoid discharge that this bank describes as a vignette nine questions earlier at `opqb-t5-166`. The lash line and the rest of the margin are spared — which is the whole difference from staphylococcal blepharitis.\n\n⚠️ **Measured gap.** `Morax` was grepped across every file in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and `content\\ophtho\\book\\`. The hits in `L6) Conjunctiva` and `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.6 are **absence registers**, not teaching — L6's own organism audit prints \"Morax / Moraxella = 0\" and records that its five bacterial-conjunctivitis slides are \"titled by DISCHARGE and MEMBRANE type only\", with \"'Angular' conjunctivitis … titled without its organism\"; the book chapter lists \"angular conjunctivitis (*Moraxella lacunata*): absent\". The only live hit in the module is `L15,16) Cornea`, which names **Moraxella** as a cause of bacterial keratitis in \"elderly, debilitated patients\". **The organism is taught; this disease is not.** What follows is *not taken from the course material*.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it causes | Why not angular |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Staphylococcus aureus** | Hordeolum externum, hordeolum internum and **ulcerative (staphylococcal) blepharitis** — book printed 72 and 74 | Takes the **whole lash line** with hard collarettes and margin ulceration; it does not confine itself to the angles or macerate skin |\n| **Koch–Weeks bacillus** (*Haemophilus aegyptius*) | Acute epidemic mucopurulent **conjunctivitis** — \"pink eye\", classically in hot dry climates | A conjunctival disease, diffusely red, not a canthal skin disease. ⚠️ Zero hits anywhere in the module outside an absence register |\n| **Pneumococci** | Acute purulent conjunctivitis, often in children, and a classical cause of the serpiginous corneal ulcer | Again diffuse conjunctival or corneal, never angle-limited |\n\n**Note on the printing.** The source sets option B as \"Koch Week's bacillus\"; the eponym is Koch–Weeks and is repaired above.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Ask *where* the blepharitis is. **Angles → Moraxella. Lash line, ulcerated → staphylococcus. Lash line, greasy scales, no ulcer → seborrhoeic** (`opqb-t5-220`). **Posterior margin, plugged orifices → meibomian gland dysfunction** (`opqb-t5-209`). Angular disease answers to topical zinc or tetracycline, which inhibit the organism's proteolytic enzyme *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Angular blepharitis at the canthi is caused by the Morax–Axenfeld diplobacillus; staphylococcus takes the lash line, and Koch–Weeks and pneumococcus cause diffuse conjunctivitis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.25 (Q175), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-176', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'All true about **senile entropion EXCEPT**:',
    options:["Can be treated by Snellen's operation",'Only in lower lid','Affected by lower lid retractor','Treated by everting'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Three of these statements come straight out of the book's entropion entry; the fourth names an operation for a different type of entropion in a different lid.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` printed 77, clinical types of entropion: \"3. **Involutional (senile) entropion**: due to **laxity of the lower lid retractors** and lateral canthal ligament.\" And, of entropion generally: \"**Most commonly affects the lower lid**\", with the further rule that \"Unlike other causes of entropion that affect lower lids only, this type [cicatricial] may affect both upper and lower lids.\" The cache calls that last sentence \"the chapter's most repeated discriminator\", because the book states it for entropion on printed 77 and again for ectropion on printed 78.\n\n| Option | Verdict | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Only in lower lid** | ✅ True. Every non-cicatricial entropion is a lower-lid disease | Book printed 77 |\n| **Affected by lower lid retractor** | ✅ True — retractor **disinsertion or laxity** is the mechanism the book names first | Book printed 77 |\n| **Treated by everting** | ✅ True. **Everting sutures** are the simplest procedure: full-thickness sutures passed from the fornix out through the skin re-attach the retractors and rotate the margin out. ⚠️ The book does not name them — its entropion treatment is the bare phrase \"Surgical treatment.\", and the cache flags that as the chapter's whole operative content, with \"no named procedure\". The bank keys the same operation again at `opqb-t5-196` |\n| **Snellen's operation** | ❌ **The exception.** A tarsal operation for **cicatricial** entropion of the **upper** lid | See below |\n\n⚠️ **Snellen's and Webster's operations are in no cached source, and that was measured** — `Snellen` was grepped across every file in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and `content\\ophtho\\book\\`, and every hit is the **visual-acuity chart** (`L1,2`, `L3,4`, `L7,8`, `L37`, book ch.1); `Webster` returns **zero hits in the whole module**. The bank's own internal evidence is nevertheless consistent, and it is worth setting out because two other questions in this topic turn on it: Snellen is keyed **false for the senile type here**, and `opqb-t5-197` keys \"Snellen's operation for treating **lower** lid\" as false as well — so the bank places it on the **upper** lid, i.e. on cicatricial entropion, the only type that takes the upper lid. *(That reconstruction is from the bank's own keys plus general knowledge, not from the course material.)*\n\n**Why the mechanism decides the operation.** Senile entropion is a problem of **laxity** — the retractors have slipped and the lid is horizontally loose — so the repair tightens or re-attaches: everting sutures, retractor repair, a lateral tarsal strip. Cicatricial entropion is a problem of **shortage** — the posterior lamella has scarred and contracted, pulling the margin in — so the repair must lengthen or rotate the tarsus, which is what a tarsal operation does. Applying a laxity repair to a shortage problem does nothing, and vice versa.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Read the EXCEPT stem as \"which of these belongs to a *different* entropion\". Lower lid, retractors, everting sutures — senile. Upper lid, scarring, tarsal surgery — cicatricial, the type of `opqb-t5-197`.\n\n**Note on the printing.** The source sets option A as the bare \"**Can be treated by Snellen**\", with no noun; \"operation\" is supplied above because the same eponym is printed in full at `opqb-t5-197` (\"Snellen's operation for treating lower lid\") and because the alternative reading — the Snellen visual-acuity chart — is not a treatment for anything.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Senile (involutional) entropion is a lower-lid disease of retractor and canthal laxity treated by everting sutures; tarsal operations such as Snellen’s belong to cicatricial entropion of the upper lid.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.26 (Q176), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-177', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'Most common **painless** swelling of eyelid:',
    options:['Chalazion','Stye','Squamous cell carcinoma','BCC'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The book supplies the superlative directly.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 73, under Incidence of chalazion: \"The **most common benign non-neoplastic lesion of the eyelids**\", and under Symptoms: \"**Gradually enlarging painless rounded nodule**.\" Painless and commonest, in the same entry, on the same page.\n\n**Why painlessness is the discriminator and not an incidental.** A chalazion is a **sterile** lipogranuloma — an obstructed meibomian gland whose retained lipid has leaked into the tarsus. There is no organism and no acute suppuration, so there is nothing to stretch the tissues quickly and nothing to inflame the nerve endings. It is firm, rubbery, sits **within the tarsal plate** away from the lid margin, and the patient usually notices it by looking in a mirror rather than by feeling it. Contrast the hordeolum, which is an acute suppurative infection and is painful for exactly the opposite reasons — the book gives it \"Painful, red, and tender swelling\" with \"dull, throbbing pain\" on printed 72.\n\n**Why each of the other three is wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it is | Why not |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Stye** (hordeolum externum) | Acute staphylococcal infection of a lash follicle and its gland of Zeis | **Painful.** The stem's one adjective excludes it outright. It is also less common than chalazion |\n| **Squamous cell carcinoma** | A lid malignancy | Painless, yes — but **uncommon**, and far less common than BCC among lid malignancies, let alone than chalazion among all lid swellings |\n| **BCC** (basal cell carcinoma) | The commonest **malignant** lid tumour | Painless, but a *malignancy*, and rare beside a chalazion. It is the commonest lid **cancer**, not the commonest lid **swelling** — the classic superlative-swap trap |\n\n⚠️ **Two of the four options are lid malignancies, and neither source teaches them.** `L5) Eyelid disease` names **not one tumour** in 47 slides — \"basal\", \"squamous\", \"sebaceous\", \"melanoma\", \"carcinoma\", \"malign\" and \"benign\" all return zero across its 170 typed words, and its ten lid-tumour slides (37–46) carry no text at all. `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5 promises \"Malignancies\" in its own objectives flowchart and in its \"3. neoplasms\" category on printed 72, and then never delivers a malignancy section anywhere on printed 70–81; the chapter's only oncological sentence is the chalazion biopsy rule on printed 73. **The book's lid malignancies live in chapter 17 (printed 214–217).** The ranking of BCC above SCC used above is therefore *not taken from the course material*.\n\n**Clinical bottom line — and it is the reason the bank keeps circling this.** A painless lid nodule in an **elderly** patient that **recurs**, destroys the lash line, or has a pearly rolled edge is a carcinoma until histology says otherwise. That is precisely why the book insists a recurrent chalazion be **biopsied** (printed 73), and why `opqb-t5-222` keys excision *with histopathology* for a chalazion that has come back three times.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Chalazion is the commonest painless lid swelling — a sterile lipogranuloma of a meibomian gland; the stye is painful, and lid carcinomas are painless but rare.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.26 (Q177), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-178', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'Complications of **anterior blepharitis EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Recurrent chalazion','Recurrent stye','Trichiasis','Madarosis'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The book prints both lists, on the same page, and the exception is the one that belongs to the *posterior* type.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` printed 74, complications of staphylococcal (ulcerative) **anterior** blepharitis:\n\n> \"o **Madarosis, trichiasis and poliosis.** / o **Recurrent styes.** / o Chronic conjunctivitis, and phlyctens.\"\n\nAnd on the very same page, the clinical picture of **posterior** blepharitis (meibomian gland dysfunction):\n\n> \"Symptoms: • Dry eye manifestations • **Recurrent chalazia**\"\n\nSo three of the four options are printed as anterior complications and the fourth is printed as a posterior one. The question is testing whether the two blepharitides have been kept apart.\n\n**Why the anatomy makes it inevitable.** Anterior blepharitis is disease of the **lash line and its follicles** — the anterior lamella. Its complications are therefore all lash complications plus the acute infection of a lash gland:\n\n| Complication | Mechanism | Book's own definition |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Madarosis** | Follicles scarred and destroyed | \"permanent loss of the lashes\", printed 74 |\n| **Trichiasis** | Scarring and **notching of the lid margin** distorts the direction of surviving lashes | named printed 74, defined printed 78 |\n| **Poliosis** | Damaged follicles lose pigment | \"premature whitening of the lashes\", printed 74 |\n| **Recurrent stye** | The staphylococci that colonise the margin repeatedly infect a lash follicle and its gland of Zeis | \"Recurrent styes\", printed 74 |\n\nThe book's own sentence for the first three reads: \"Lashes fall with scarring and notching of the lid margin leading to **madarosis** (permanent loss of the lashes), **trichiasis** and **poliosis** (premature whitening of the lashes)\". The complications atlas of four photographs across printed 74 shows madarosis, trichiasis, a recurrent stye and marginal keratitis — and the cache notes that **marginal keratitis appears in the whole chapter only in that caption**.\n\n**Recurrent chalazion is a posterior problem** because a chalazion is an obstructed **meibomian** gland, and the meibomian glands live in the **tarsus** — the posterior lamella. MGD is precisely the state in which meibomian secretion becomes inspissated and the orifices plug, so chalazia recur. `opqb-t5-209` keys the gland; `opqb-t5-193` and `opqb-t5-222` work the chalazion itself.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Recurrent **styes** point to anterior blepharitis; recurrent **chalazia** point to posterior blepharitis. Both are why the book says \"Prevent recurrence by treating any associated local disease (e.g., blepharitis)\" on printed 72 — the rule `opqb-t5-219` keys.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Anterior blepharitis complicates as madarosis, trichiasis, poliosis and recurrent styes; recurrent chalazia belong to posterior blepharitis (meibomian gland dysfunction).',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.26 (Q178), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-179', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'All the following is used in treatment of **trichiasis EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Argon laser','Cryotherapy','Electrolysis',"Webster's operation"],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"Treatment of Trichiasis\n**1. repeated epilation of the offending lashes.**\n**2. electrolysis of the lash follicles.**\n**3. cryotherapy.**\n**4. or surgery.**\n\n---\n\n*(The list above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The book prints the same four, in the same order, and marks two of them.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 78: \"1. Repeated epilation of the offending lashes. **(Temporary → Tutorial )** / 2. electrolysis of the lash follicles / 3. cryotherapy. / 4. surgery. **(mm graft in gray line → Tutorial)**\". The cache warns that the tutorial marker there wraps two of the five treatments, so reading past it deletes numbered list items rather than footnotes — and that the only description of what the operation *is* (a **mucous-membrane graft in the grey line**) lives inside the marker.\n\n**Every option except the last destroys or redirects the offending follicle:**\n\n| Option | How it works | Where it fits |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Argon laser** | Photocoagulates the follicle through the margin, lash by lash | ⚠️ **Not in the printed box and not in the book** — but keyed as a correct treatment. It is a standard modality for a **few** scattered lashes, the same niche as electrolysis. *(Not taken from the course material.)* |\n| **Cryotherapy** | A freeze–thaw cycle kills lash follicles, which are more cold-sensitive than the surrounding tissue | Box item 3. Used for **many** lashes over a segment — which is why `opqb-t5-223` keys it for ten lashes in the outer third of a lid |\n| **Electrolysis** | A fine needle into each follicle, current destroys it | Box item 2. Definitive but slow, so it suits **few** lashes |\n| **Webster's operation** | ❌ **The exception** | See below |\n\n⚠️ **Webster's operation appears nowhere in the course material — measured.** `Webster` was grepped case-insensitively across every file in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and `content\\ophtho\\book\\` and returns **zero hits in the entire module**. What can be said from the bank's own keys is that it treats Webster as *not* a trichiasis treatment here and as *not* the main treatment of cicatricial entropion at `opqb-t5-197` — i.e. as a lid-malposition operation belonging somewhere other than these two places. Beyond that, nothing is claimed. *(Not taken from the course material.)*\n\n**Note on the printing.** The source sets option D as the bare surname \"**Webster**\", with no noun; \"operation\" is supplied above, because the three options it stands beside are all named procedures.\n\n**The one distinction that makes this question clinical.** Trichiasis is treated by attacking the **lashes**, because the **lid margin is normal** — that is the book's own discriminator on printed 78 and the key at `opqb-t5-208`. Entropion is treated by attacking the **margin**, because the margin is what has rotated. An operation that repositions a lid is therefore the wrong instrument for trichiasis, and an operation that kills follicles is the wrong instrument for entropion.",
    objective:'Trichiasis is treated by destroying or redirecting the offending lash follicles — epilation, electrolysis, cryotherapy, argon laser, or grey-line graft surgery — not by a lid-repositioning operation.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.26 (Q179), key p.31 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-180', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'The following statement is **true** regarding a **chalazion**:',
    options:['Usually, it is painful','Usually, it should be opened through a skin incision','When it is large, it can cause diminution of visual acuity','It usually points in front of the gray line'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A big enough chalazion presses on the globe and distorts the cornea.** The lid is a firm structure resting on a soft one; a nodule of a few millimetres within the tarsus indents the cornea beneath it and flattens it in that meridian, inducing **astigmatism**. Vision blurs, and it blurs by an optical mechanism, not by covering the pupil. In children a large upper-lid chalazion can be an amblyogenic cause for the same reason. *(The astigmatic mechanism is not taken from the course material — the book's chalazion entry, printed 73, gives no visual consequence at all, and `L5` slide 18 photographs five chalazia with no words.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and each is sourced:**\n\n| Option | Verdict | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Usually, it is painful** | ❌ Painless is the defining symptom — \"Gradually enlarging **painless** rounded nodule\" | Book printed 73. It is also the key at `opqb-t5-177` and the discriminator at `opqb-t5-211` |\n| **Opened through a skin incision** | ❌ The chalazion is opened from the **conjunctival** side — \"Incision and curettage through the **conjunctival side**\" | Book printed 73; the same route is keyed at `opqb-t5-193`. `L5` slide 19 photographs the chalazion clamp applied to the **everted** lid, which is the same instruction in a picture |\n| **Points in front of the gray line** | ❌ It points **behind** it, on the tarsal conjunctiva | See below |\n\n**Why the grey line settles option D.** The book defines it on printed 71: \"the grey line which is an **avascular line** marking the separation of the lid surgically into two leaflets — **1. An anterior leaflet** composed of the skin, subcutaneous tissue and the orbicularis muscle; **2. A posterior leaflet** composed of the **tarsal plate and the palpebral conjunctiva**.\" A chalazion is a lipogranuloma **inside the tarsus**, so it belongs to the posterior leaflet and points **behind** the grey line, onto the palpebral conjunctiva. `L5` slide 18 shows exactly that in two of its five pictures — the lid everted, \"the chalazion pointing on the **CONJUNCTIVAL surface** as a raised yellow-red lesion\".\n\nThe lesion that points **in front of** the grey line is the **stye** — an anterior-lamella infection of a lash follicle, which the book describes as \"pointing anteriorly **through the skin** on the lid margin usually with a lash at its apex\" (printed 72). That is the sign in the `opqb-t5-210` vignette.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The grey line is the surgical watershed of the lid, and it sorts the two commonest lid lumps: **in front of it → stye, drain through skin; behind it → chalazion, incise through conjunctiva.** The corpus asks the landmark itself at `opmcq-c1-31` and the incision at `opmcq-c1-1`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A large chalazion blurs vision by inducing astigmatism; it is painless, points behind the grey line onto the conjunctiva, and is opened from the conjunctival side.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.26 (Q180), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-181', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'Considering the **management of ptosis**:',
    options:['In paralytic ptosis, surgery should correct the ptosis before the strabismus','All congenital unilateral ptosis cases should be operated upon immediately','Mechanical ptosis necessitates a levator resection surgery','The frontalis suspension is needed if the levator function is absent'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Only D restates a rule the course actually prints.** `L5) Eyelid disease` slide 9 — one of only two slides in the 47 carrying body text — reads: \"**Frontalis brow suspension. Main indications: · Severe ptosis with poor levator function ( 4 mm or less ) · Marcus Gunn jaw-winking syndrome**\", with the caption \"Attachment of tarsus to frontalis muscle with sling\". The book's surgical plate on printed 76 heads the same panel \"**Frontalis sling / Poor levator function**\". If the levator does not work at all, the lid must be lifted by a muscle that does, and frontalis is the only candidate — the mechanism already worked at `opqb-t5-169`.\n\n**Why each of the other three fails, and each failure is a rule worth having:**\n\n**A — \"correct the ptosis before the strabismus\" reverses the order.** In a paralytic ptosis, and above all in a third-nerve palsy, the eye is also **deviated** and the cornea is **at risk**. Lifting the lid over an eye that is turned down and out converts a covered, comfortable eye into an exposed, diplopic one. So the **squint and the ocular surface are addressed first**, and the lid last. The same logic makes a ptosis over an anaesthetic or non-closing eye a positively *protective* thing that surgery should be slow to remove. *(Not taken from the course material — neither source prints a sequencing rule.)*\n\n**B — \"operated upon immediately\" is wrong in both directions.** The indication for early surgery is not the unilaterality but whether the lid is **covering the visual axis**. If it is, the child is at risk of **deprivation amblyopia** and should be operated early — the fact `opqb-t5-187` keys. If it is not, surgery waits until the levator function and lid height can be measured reliably and the child is old enough for the result to last. \"All … immediately\" collapses that distinction. ⚠️ Amblyopia is named **nowhere** in `L5` (measured across its 170 typed words) and nowhere in the book's ptosis section.\n\n**C — mechanical ptosis is treated by removing the weight, not by shortening the levator.** The book defines the type on printed 76: \"**Mechanical:** Increased lid weight from a **lid mass, lid oedema or palpebral vernal catarrh**\", and its treatment line says \"Treatment of the **cause** e.g., mechanical ptosis\" — the only aetiology the book singles out by name in the treatment list. `L5` slide 14 photographs it: an infant whose whole upper-outer lid is occupied by a lobulated red-purple vascular mass that \"drags the lid down so the eye is completely occluded\". Resecting that child's levator would leave the mass, the ptosis and the amblyopia exactly where they were.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Ptosis surgery has one measurement and one question. The measurement is **levator function** (`opqb-t5-174`, `opqb-t5-217`); the question is **why is the lid down** — because a mechanical, myasthenic or paralytic ptosis is treated by treating its cause, and only a dystrophic or aponeurotic one is treated by operating on the lid itself.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A frontalis sling is required when levator function is absent; mechanical ptosis is treated by removing the cause, congenital ptosis is operated for the visual axis rather than on principle, and in paralytic ptosis the squint and cornea come first.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.26 (Q181), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-182', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Lagophthalmos** is:',
    options:['Wide palpebral fissure.','Lid lag.','Inability to close the palpebral fissure.','Incomplete opening of the palpebral fissure'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The book gives the definition in one sentence.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 80: \"It is the **inability to close the eyelids completely** with the palpebral fissure remaining partially open during a **blink and sleep**. The ocular surface become subject to damage from **exposure**.\"\n\n**Why the definition insists on *closure* and not on *width*.** The whole clinical danger of lagophthalmos is that the cornea is uncovered when it should be covered — during the blink that resurfaces the tear film, and through the night. Exposure keratopathy therefore starts in the **lower third** of the cornea, the strip the lids fail to reach, and progresses to ulceration and scarring. The `opqb-t5-201` vignette is that sequence: facial weakness, then a dry cornea \"mildly ulcerated **inferiorly**\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and D is the sharp one:**\n\n| Option | What it names | Why not |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Wide palpebral fissure** | A *static* measurement, which is what **lid retraction** produces. The book's retraction section, printed 76: \"The sclera above the limbus should not be visible in the resting position … Lid retraction causes the sclera to be visible ('scleral show')\" | A wide fissure with a lid that still closes is retraction, not lagophthalmos — although retraction is one of the things that *causes* lagophthalmos, which is why the book adds \"The eye may become dry due to exposure of the cornea\" |\n| **Lid lag** | The upper lid failing to follow the globe **on downgaze**, a *dynamic* sign of thyroid eye disease | A movement sign in downgaze, not a closure failure |\n| **Incomplete opening** | ❌ The exact inversion of the answer — this describes **ptosis** | The trap is a single word, *opening* for *closing*. Read the option, not the shape of it |\n\n**The five aetiologies, from the book's own list on printed 80–81** — worth having because the bank asks the causes at `opqb-t5-201`, `-206` and `-216`:\n\n1. **Facial nerve dysfunction (paralytic)** — trauma, cerebrovascular accident, **Bell's palsy**, cerebellopontine-angle tumours\n2. **Cicatricial** — excessive scar tissue or excessive lid removal at surgery\n3. **Physiologic (nocturnal)** — common, and partly protected by **Bell's phenomenon**\n4. **Medication effect** — sedatives and neuromuscular blockers in ICU, incidence \"21-75%\" (the chapter's only percentage)\n5. **Functional (GA, unconsciousness)** — ⚠️ tutorial-marked in the original; the cache warns that skipping the marker deletes a whole numbered aetiology\n\n`L5` heads slide 15 \"Lagophthalmos / Paralytic\" and slide 16 \"Lagophthalmos / Structural\" and prints no other word on either — the classification is two headings and two photographs. ⚠️ **\"Bell\" returns zero across all 170 of that deck's typed words**, although slide 15 photographs Bell's phenomenon happening.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The corpus already works the causes as an EXCEPT question at `opqb-t3-115`, where the answer turns on the fifth nerve causing **neurotrophic** rather than exposure keratopathy — read the two together.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Lagophthalmos is failure of complete lid closure during blink and sleep, exposing the lower cornea; a wide fissure is retraction, lid lag is a downgaze sign, and incomplete opening is ptosis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.26 (Q182), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-183', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Ptosis cannot** be caused by:',
    options:['Third nerve palsy.',"Horner's syndrome.",'Large chalazion of the lower lid.','Trauma.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The trap is one word: *lower*.** Ptosis is by definition a drooping of the **upper** lid — book printed 75: \"Drooping of the **upper eyelid** below its normal position, so that it covers more than 2 mm of the superior limbus in the primary position.\" A mass in the lower lid cannot pull the upper lid down. A mass in the **upper** lid can, and does, and the book gives it a numbered aetiology of its own.\n\n**Three of the four are on the book's own list of ptosis aetiologies, printed 76:**\n\n| Option | The book's type | The book's words |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Third nerve palsy** | 2. **Paralytic** | \"Due to III nerve (oculomotor nerve) palsy\" — the complete ptosis with a down-and-out eye and a dilated pupil, keyed at `opqb-t5-170` |\n| **Horner's syndrome** | 4. **Sympathetic** | \"As in Horner's syndrome (affecting Muller's muscle)\" — the partial ~2 mm ptosis with miosis, keyed at `opqb-t5-168` |\n| **Trauma** | 7. **Traumatic** (and 5, aponeurotic, which lists \"advancing age, **trauma**, or ocular surgery\") | A lid injury can produce ptosis immediately by oedema and haematoma, and permanently by disinserting the levator aponeurosis — the mechanism worked in the shipped `opqb-t3-120` |\n\n**The fourth type the option is *pretending* to be.** Book printed 76, aetiology 6: \"**Mechanical:** Increased lid weight from a **lid mass**, lid oedema or palpebral vernal catarrh.\" A large chalazion of the **upper** lid is a textbook mechanical ptosis, and `L5) Eyelid disease` slide 14 photographs the extreme version — an infant whose upper lid carries a large lobulated vascular mass that \"drags the lid down so the eye is completely occluded\". Change the option's *lower* to *upper* and it becomes a correct answer, which is exactly why it is the one to eliminate.\n\n**What a large lower-lid chalazion *does* do.** It can indent the globe and induce astigmatism, blurring vision (the mechanism of `opqb-t5-180`); it can cause a mechanical **ectropion** or entropion by its weight; but it does not move the upper lid.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** When a stem names a lid, read it. This topic uses the same trick twice — *lower* here, and *white line* for *grey line* at `opqb-t5-192`. Both questions are answerable purely by noticing which structure has been named.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Ptosis is a drooping of the upper lid: third-nerve palsy, Horner’s syndrome and trauma all cause it, but only an UPPER-lid mass produces mechanical ptosis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.26 (Q183), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-184', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'The following sign is **NOT** a manifestation of **aponeurotic ptosis**:',
    options:['High upper lid skin creases.','Good levator function.','Thinning the lid above the tarsus.','Increased lid weight.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Increased lid weight is the definition of the *mechanical* type, and the two aetiologies are being separated.** Book printed 76 lists them one after the other:\n\n> \"5. **Aponeurotic (Senile):** – Due to **disinsertion or dehiscence of the levator aponeurosis**. – Associated with advancing age, trauma, or ocular surgery – Typically shows a **good levator function**.\"\n> \"6. **Mechanical:** • **Increased lid weight** from a lid mass, lid oedema or palpebral vernal catarrh.\"\n\nThe wording of option D is lifted straight out of aetiology 6.\n\n**Why the other three all follow from one lesion.** In aponeurotic ptosis the levator *muscle* is normal; what has failed is the **aponeurosis**, its tendon, which has stretched, thinned or pulled off the tarsus. Everything follows:\n\n| Sign | Why the disinsertion produces it |\n|---|---|\n| **Good levator function** | The muscle still contracts through its full excursion. The force simply is not transmitted to the tarsus. This is *the* discriminator from congenital ptosis, where function is poor — and it is why the operation here is a **levator (aponeurosis) repair**, not a sling |\n| **High upper lid skin creases** | The crease is made where the aponeurosis inserts into the skin. If the aponeurosis has slipped **backwards and upwards**, so does the crease — often to 12 mm or more, against a normal 8–10 mm. Option A therefore reads as *true* and is not the exception |\n| **Thinning of the lid above the tarsus** | With the aponeurosis dehisced and the preaponeurotic fat sunk back, the pretarsal lid becomes thin enough to see the iris through in extreme cases, and the sulcus above it hollows out |\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` slide 12 is headed \"Ptosis / Senile (Aponeurotic)\" and photographs precisely that constellation with no words at all: \"**high, deepened upper lid creases**, very thin upper lids with the **sulcus hollowed**, redundant skin, and both lid margins low across the pupils\". The book's own figure plate on printed 76 captions a clinical photograph \"**Aponeurotic ptosis / Good levator function**\", pairing the aetiology with its levator function in the caption. *(The 8–10 mm normal crease height above is not taken from the course material — the book prints no crease measurement, and states that its 2 mm limbal rule is the chapter's only quantitative criterion.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Aponeurotic ptosis is the ptosis of the elderly, of contact-lens wearers, of chronic lid rubbing, and — as `opqb-t5-194` keys with a printed explanation box — of **cataract surgery**, where the lid speculum stretches the aponeurosis. Good function, high crease, thin lid: repair the aponeurosis.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Aponeurotic ptosis shows a high lid crease, a thinned pretarsal lid and good levator function because the tendon, not the muscle, has failed; increased lid weight defines mechanical ptosis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.26 (Q184), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-185', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Ectropion** can be due to:',
    options:['Scarring of the conjunctiva.','Atrophic eyes.','Injury to the third nerve.','Injury to the seventh nerve.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The seventh nerve supplies orbicularis oculi, and orbicularis is what holds the lower lid up against the globe.** Book printed 78, clinical types of ectropion: \"3. **Paralytic: VII (facial) nerve palsy**.\" Lose the tone of the pretarsal and preseptal orbicularis and the lower lid, unsupported, falls away from the eye under its own weight.\n\n**The book's four types, and where the options sit against them** (printed 78):\n\n| Type | Mechanism |\n|---|---|\n| 1. **Mechanical** | A mass or oedema dragging the lid out |\n| 2. **Senile (involutional)** | \"due to a **horizontal laxity** of the lower eyelid\" — the commonest, and the answer keyed in the shipped `opqb-t1-17` |\n| 3. **Paralytic** | **VII nerve palsy** ✅ |\n| 4. **Cicatricial** | \"The **only type** that can involve both upper and lower lids\" — from burns, eczema, post-operative scars, lacerated lid wounds. Note that it is scarring of the **skin**, the anterior lamella, pulling the lid out |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and two of them are entropion mechanisms:**\n\n| Option | What it actually causes |\n|---|---|\n| **Scarring of the conjunctiva** | **Cicatricial ENTROPION** — the exact opposite malposition. Book printed 77: \"due to a **shortening of the conjunctival surface** of the eyelid, causing it to **rotate inward**\", from trauma, Stevens–Johnson syndrome or **trachoma**. Shortage of the *posterior* lamella turns the lid IN; shortage of the *anterior* lamella turns it OUT. That single rule separates cicatricial entropion from cicatricial ectropion |\n| **Atrophic eyes** | An atrophic or phthisical globe gives no support to the lids, and the classic result is **entropion** — the book lists \"lack of lid support by the globe as in **enophthalmos**\" on printed 75, though it lists it there as a cause of pseudoptosis |\n| **Injury to the third nerve** | **Ptosis** — the levator is III-supplied. Nothing to do with the lower lid |\n\n**The clinical consequences the book attaches to ectropion, printed 77**, and they explain both the symptoms and the treatment: \"exposure of the palpebral conjunctiva, which becomes **dry and irritated**\"; \"difficulty closing the eye completely, resulting in **dryness of the cornea**\"; and \"The eyelids cannot effectively push tears toward the tear drain, resulting in **epiphora**\". `L5` photographs the endpoint on slide 32 — a lower lid everted so long that the exposed tarsal conjunctiva has become \"THICKENED AND WHITISH — a keratinised band\".\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A facial palsy produces three lid problems at once and all three are asked in this topic: **paralytic ectropion** here, **lagophthalmos** at `opqb-t5-201`, and **exposure keratopathy** at `opqb-t5-216`. `L5` slide 31, headed \"Ectropion / Paralytic\", photographs all three in one frame — and names none of them.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Paralytic ectropion follows a VII nerve palsy that denervates orbicularis; conjunctival scarring and an unsupported globe cause entropion, and III nerve injury causes ptosis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.26 (Q185), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-186', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Madarosis** is:',
    options:['Lid margin eczema.','Loss of eye lashes.','Distortion of eye lashes.','Extra row of eye lashes.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The book defines it inside a parenthesis, and the parenthesis is the answer.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 74, complications of staphylococcal blepharitis: \"Lashes fall with scarring and notching of the lid margin leading to **madarosis (permanent loss of the lashes)**, trichiasis and poliosis (premature whitening of the lashes).\"\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` gives it a whole slide — slide 24, headed \"Madarosis\", with one photograph and no other word: an eye in which \"**BOTH LID MARGINS ARE COMPLETELY BALD**: not a single lash remains on the upper or the lower lid\", the margins pink and smooth, \"no crust, no scale and no ulceration\". That last clause matters: the picture is of the **end state**, after the inflammation that destroyed the follicles has burnt out.\n\n**Why lashes are lost.** Chronic staphylococcal blepharitis ulcerates the lid margin; the ulceration scars; scarring destroys the lash follicles. Loss is therefore **permanent** — the word the book chooses — which distinguishes it from the temporary lash loss of an acute episode or of mechanical rubbing.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and all three are terms this topic keys elsewhere:**\n\n| Option | What it names | Where this bank uses it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Lid margin eczema** | A skin condition of the margin, not a lash term. Book printed 79 lists \"Skin eczema with scaling and erythema of the skin of the eyelids\" only as a cause of **allergic lid swelling**, and printed 78 lists eczema as a cause of **cicatricial ectropion** | — |\n| **Distortion of eye lashes** | **Trichiasis** — \"the eyelashes are misdirected toward the eye\", book printed 78 | keyed at `opqb-t5-208`, treated at `opqb-t5-179`, `-221`, `-223` |\n| **Extra row of eye lashes** | **Distichiasis** — and this bank keyed exactly that phrasing twenty-one questions earlier | `opqb-t5-165` |\n\n**The four lash words, together, because the topic recycles them as each other's distractors:**\n\n| Word | Meaning | Book page |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Madarosis** | Permanent **loss** of lashes | printed 74 |\n| **Poliosis** | Premature **whitening** of lashes | printed 74 |\n| **Trichiasis** | **Misdirection** of a normal row, normal margin | printed 78 |\n| **Distichiasis** | An **extra** posterior row | ⚠️ absent from both sources — see `opqb-t5-165` |\n\n**Note on the printing.** The source sets option D as \"**Extra raw eye lashes.**\" — *raw* for *row*, and the connecting *of* dropped. Repaired above to \"Extra row of eye lashes\", the phrasing this bank itself uses for distichiasis at `opqb-t5-165` (\"Accessory row of eyelashes\").\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Madarosis has one sinister differential the sources do not print: **lash loss over a lid-margin lesion is a malignancy sign**, because a tumour destroys follicles as it grows. `L5` photographs lash loss over margin lesions on four of its ten silent tumour slides and names none of them *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Madarosis is permanent loss of the eyelashes from follicle-destroying margin disease; poliosis is whitening, trichiasis misdirection and distichiasis an extra row.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.27 (Q186), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-187', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Congenital ptosis** is:',
    options:['Usually unilateral.','Due mainly to faulty development of superior rectus.','Indicated for surgery if the lid covers the pupil preventing normal vision development.','Rarely associated with paralysis of extraocular muscle'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The keyed statement is the one that decides management: a lid across the visual axis in a developing eye is an amblyopia risk, and that risk — not appearance, not age — is what brings surgery forward.** Deprivation amblyopia sets in during the sensitive period of visual development, and unlike the ptosis itself it is not correctable later. So a severe congenital ptosis occluding the pupil is operated in early childhood, while a mild one that clears the axis waits until levator function and lid height can be measured reliably.\n\n⚠️ **Amblyopia is a measured gap.** `amblyop` returns **zero** across all 170 typed words of `L5) Eyelid disease` — the deck's own audit records that its three hits are the transcriber's notes marking the absence, on slides 6, 14 and 37, all three of which photograph a lid occluding a pupil. The book's ptosis section, printed 75–76, prints no visual consequence either. The reasoning above is therefore *not taken from the course material*.\n\n⚠️ **Option A also reads as true, and the key is recorded as printed, not disputed.** Congenital ptosis is unilateral in the substantial majority of cases, and `L5` slide 6 photographs four children of whom three are unilateral and one bilateral. The bank keys **C**, and C is the statement that is both true and *actionable* — the defensible reading of a single-best stem. The discrepancy is noted here rather than argued.\n\n**Why B and D are wrong, and why they are not arbitrary distractors:**\n\n**B — the muscle is the LEVATOR, not the superior rectus.** Book printed 76, aetiology 1: \"Congenital … Due to **lack of development of levator muscle**, resulting in a smooth lid with loss of tarsal fold [**poor levator function**].\" The dystrophic levator is replaced by fibroadipose tissue, which is why function is poor and why the lid also **lags on downgaze** — the fibrotic muscle cannot relax.\n\n**D — the association with extraocular muscle weakness is COMMON, not rare.** The levator and the superior rectus share an embryological origin, so a dysplastic levator very often comes with a weak superior rectus on the same side; this is also why option B is a half-truth rather than nonsense. In practice that means **checking elevation before operating**, and it feeds the rule discussed at `opqb-t5-181`, that in a ptosis with a squint the eye is dealt with before the lid. *(Not taken from the course material — neither source states the association.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In a child with ptosis, three things decide everything: **is the pupil covered** (amblyopia — operate early), **what is the levator function** (`opqb-t5-174`, `opqb-t5-217`), and **is the eye moving normally**. Everything else can wait.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Congenital ptosis is a levator dystrophy with poor levator function; the surgical indication is occlusion of the visual axis and the risk of deprivation amblyopia.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.27 (Q187), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-188', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A **hordeolum internum**:',
    options:['It is an inflammation of the gland of Zeiss.','It is treated surgically once it is diagnosed.','Points mainly in the palpebral conjunctiva.','It is a precancerous condition.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The internal hordeolum is an infected MEIBOMIAN gland, and meibomian glands open on the posterior lid margin — so it points backwards, onto the tarsal conjunctiva.** The book's own treatment rule turns on that fact, printed 72: \"Topical antibiotic eye drops, or ointments are prescribed **only if the lesion is pointing at the palpebral conjunctiva**.\"\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` slide 20 is headed \"Infected Chalazion / Hordeolum Internum\" and its two photographs show a swelling \"within the substance of the lid, **well BEHIND the lash line**, with a pale pointing centre\", and a lower lid with \"a yellow pointing abscess **in the middle of the lid** … deep to the margin rather than at the lash root\". The deck prints no words; the contrast with slide 17's stye is carried entirely by the pairing of the two headings.\n\n**The book's comparison table, printed 72, answers every option:**\n\n| | Hordeolum externum (stye) | Hordeolum internum |\n|---|---|---|\n| Gland | \"the follicle of the lash or the associated **gland of Zeiss**\" | \"Acute inflammation of **Meibomian glands**\" |\n| Organism | *Staphylococcus aureus* | *Staphylococcus aureus* |\n| Where it points | **Anteriorly through the skin** on the lid margin, \"usually with a lash at its apex\" | **Posteriorly**, on the palpebral conjunctiva |\n| Incidence | Common in children and young adults | \"the **second most common** infection of the eyelid\" |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Gland of Zeiss** | That is the **externum** — the stye. `opqb-t5-212` keys it. The internum is meibomian |\n| **Treated surgically once diagnosed** | The direct opposite of the book's rule: \"It should **NEVER** be incised during active inflammation **UNLESS** it forms an abscess\" (printed 72, with NEVER and UNLESS underlined in the original). First-line treatment is warm compresses with gentle massage, topical antibiotic if it points conjunctivally, and oral antibiotics with analgesics |\n| **A precancerous condition** | An acute bacterial infection is not premalignant. The one place malignancy enters this chapter is the **recurrent chalazion** rule on printed 73 — biopsy to exclude meibomian gland carcinoma — which is a rule about *recurrence*, not about acute infection, and which `opqb-t5-222` keys |\n\n**Note on the printing.** A stray underline sits after \"diagnosed\" in option B of the source — a typesetting artefact; no word is underlined.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Where a lid lesion **points** tells you which lamella it is in, and therefore how to drain it: **anteriorly through skin → stye** (or epilate the lash); **posteriorly onto conjunctiva → internal hordeolum or chalazion**, and never incise one that is acutely inflamed unless it has formed an abscess.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Hordeolum internum is acute staphylococcal infection of a meibomian gland pointing on the palpebral conjunctiva; it is not a Zeis lesion, is never incised during active inflammation unless an abscess forms, and is not premalignant.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.27 (Q188), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-189', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'**Surgical emphysema of the lid** is usually due to a fracture of:',
    options:['Zygomatic bone.','Frontal bone.','Palatine bone.','Ethmoidal bone.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Air gets into the lid because a paper-thin bone between the orbit and an air-filled sinus has broken, and the thinnest of them is the ethmoid.** The **lamina papyracea** — the ethmoid's orbital plate, forming the medial orbital wall — is the flimsiest wall of the orbit, and behind it lie the ethmoidal air cells. Fracture it and any rise in nasal pressure, above all **blowing the nose**, drives air out of the cells into the orbit and forwards into the loose subcutaneous tissue of the lids, where it produces a swollen lid that **crackles under the finger** (crepitus).\n\n**Both sources put the medial wall beside the floor and both warn about the nose.** `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.3, blow-out fracture: \"The fracture most frequently involves **the floor of the orbit** … Sometimes, **the medial orbital wall** may also be fractured. **Those thin walls of the orbit separate the orbital cavity from the surrounding nasal sinuses.**\" Its clinical picture opens with \"**Infection** can spread from the nasal sinuses to the orbit with **nose blowing**\", it lists \"Ecchymosis, oedema, and **subcutaneous emphysema**\" among the periocular signs, and its management begins \"Patients should be advised to **avoid blowing the nose**.\" `L25) Trauma I` slide 18 is headed \"**Blunt trauma: Surgical emphysema**\" — the deck's only named slide on it — with a four-panel figure of clinical lid swelling and axial and coronal CT showing orbital air; slide 20 lists \"ECCHYMOSIS, OEDEMA, and ± SUBCUTANEOUS EMPHYSEMA\" as the presentation of an orbital floor fracture, and slide 23 lists \"± Surgical emphysema\" among the CT findings. **The deck places emphysema immediately before the blow-out slides — it treats it as the presenting sign of the fracture.**\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Its relation to the orbit | Why it does not give lid emphysema |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Zygomatic bone** | Forms the **lateral wall and the inferolateral rim** — the thickest, strongest part of the orbital margin | It is solid bone, not a sinus wall. A zygomatic complex fracture gives a flattened cheek, a step at the rim and infraorbital numbness, not air in the lid |\n| **Frontal bone** | Forms the **roof** and the superior rim; the frontal sinus lies within it | It *can* let air out if the sinus is breached, but it is thick bone and such fractures are far less common than medial-wall fractures. Not the usual cause |\n| **Palatine bone** | Contributes only a small **orbital process** to the posterior floor | It is neither thin at the orbit nor a wall of an air cell in the way the lamina papyracea is |\n\n*(The bone-by-bone reasoning above is not taken from the course material — neither source names a specific bone for lid emphysema; both name the medial wall and the floor as the thin walls adjoining the sinuses.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Lid emphysema after blunt orbital trauma **is a fractured medial wall until CT says otherwise**, and the single most useful thing to say to the patient is **do not blow your nose** — the manoeuvre that can convert a small leak into a tense orbit and an infected one. ⚠️ *Filed under Trauma; the bank prints it in its Eyelid topic because the sign shows in the lid (secondary chapter: Lid).*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Surgical emphysema of the lid follows fracture of the ethmoid’s lamina papyracea, the thin medial orbital wall against the ethmoidal air cells; the patient must not blow the nose.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.27 (Q189), key p.31' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-190', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'The **normal range of action of the levator palpebrae superioris** is:',
    options:['2 – 4 mm.','28 – 30 mm.','More than 30 mm.','15 – 18 mm.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Levator function is the total excursion of the upper lid margin from full downgaze to full upgaze with the brow held, and normal is about 15–18 mm.**\n\n⚠️ **This number is not in the course material, and that was measured rather than assumed.** `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5's own audit of what it does not print reads: \"Ptosis MEASUREMENTS beyond the 2 mm definition — ABSENT. No margin-reflex distance (MRD1/MRD2), no palpebral fissure height in mm, **no levator function graded in millimetres**\", and it records that the only numbers anywhere on printed 70–81 are \"5 layers\", \"two leaflets\", \"more than 2 mm\", \"4th to 5th decades of life\" and \"21-75%\". `L5) Eyelid disease` is no better: the digits in all 170 of its typed words are exactly **5** and **4**, both on slides 8 and 9, and both are surgical thresholds — \"levator function is at least **5 mm**\" and \"poor levator function ( **4 mm or less** )\". So the module teaches where the *cut-offs* are and never says what *normal* is. The value below is *not taken from the course material*.\n\n**How the measurement is made** — and `L5` slide 7 photographs it without a word of explanation: the examiner's **thumb is pressed firmly on the brow** to block frontalis (otherwise the brow lifts the lid and the reading is falsely high), a millimetre ruler is held vertically against the upper lid, the lid margin is zeroed in **full downgaze**, and the excursion is read off in **full upgaze**. The slide's photograph carries a large white numeral **5** in its corner — the reading being demonstrated, i.e. a poor one.\n\n**The scale the thresholds sit on:**\n\n| Levator function | Grade | Operation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **15–18 mm** | **Normal** | — |\n| ≥ 8 mm | Good | Levator resection (`opqb-t5-174`) |\n| 5–7 mm | Fair | Levator resection, larger amount |\n| ≤ 4 mm | Poor | Frontalis sling (`opqb-t5-169`, `opqb-t5-181`) |\n\n**Why the distractors are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **2 – 4 mm** | This is *poor* function — the sling range of `L5` slide 9, and roughly the amount of lift **Müller's muscle** alone contributes (the muscle lost in Horner's syndrome, `opqb-t5-168`). It is a real number in the wrong role |\n| **28 – 30 mm** | Larger than the whole vertical palpebral fissure and larger than the corneal diameter. Anatomically impossible |\n| **More than 30 mm** | Wider than the eye itself |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Two numbers run this whole topic and they are easily confused: **2 mm** is how much of the superior limbus a normal upper lid covers (the ptosis definition, book printed 75, and the key at `opqb-t5-192`), and **15–18 mm** is how far that lid travels. The first is a position, the second a movement.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Normal levator function — lid excursion from full downgaze to full upgaze with the brow fixed — is about 15–18 mm; 5 mm and 4 mm are the surgical thresholds, not the normal range.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.27 (Q190), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-191', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'**Ectropion uveae** is seen in:',
    options:['Acute uveitis.','Acute angle closure glaucoma.','With ectropion of the lower lid.','Neovascular glaucoma.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Ectropion uveae is not a lid sign at all — it is the pigmented posterior surface of the iris dragged round the pupil margin onto the front of the iris, and a contracting fibrovascular membrane is what drags it.** In **neovascular glaucoma**, new vessels grow over the anterior iris surface (**rubeosis iridis**) on a sheet of myofibroblastic tissue. As that sheet contracts it does two things at once: it pulls the peripheral iris up onto the trabecular meshwork, closing the angle by synechiae, and it pulls the **posterior pigment epithelium** of the iris forwards around the pupil, where it shows as a dark velvety collar. Ectropion uveae and a closed angle therefore have one cause and appear together.\n\n**`L12) Glaucoma 2` gives the mechanism verbatim**, under secondary angle-closure by anterior pulling: \"**Neovascular Glaucoma — Neovascular membrane grows over the trabecular meshwork and contracts pulling the peripheral iris over the trabeculum**\". `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 9 is headed \"**Rubeosis iridis**\" and photographs new vessels on the iris surface; the deck's own audit records that the slide \"prints no causes, no consequence and no treatment\". `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` supplies the commonest cause — \"**Rubeosis irides in 50%**\" after ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion — and instructs \"Look for rubeosis and angle new vessels\". `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.10 lists rubeosis iridis among the iris abnormalities and photographs it, and the cache flags that the six named iris conditions there are \"LISTED and PICTURED but NOT DEFINED anywhere in the chapter\".\n\n⚠️ **The term \"ectropion uveae\" itself is a measured gap** — grepped case-insensitively across every file in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and `content\\ophtho\\book\\`, it returns **zero hits in the whole module**. The definition and the traction mechanism above are *not taken from the course material*; the rubeosis and the angle closure it produces are.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it does to the iris |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute uveitis** | Produces **posterior synechiae** — iris stuck to the anterior lens capsule — giving a festooned, irregular pupil, and it is the cause of *seclusio* and *occlusio pupillae*. Book ch.10 pictures and names posterior synechiae. Inflammatory adhesion, not pigment eversion |\n| **Acute angle closure glaucoma** | The angle closes by **pupil block** — aqueous cannot pass the pupil, the peripheral iris bows forward. The iris ends up in the angle without any membrane pulling it, and the acute attack leaves a **mid-dilated, vertically oval, fixed pupil** and sometimes iris atrophy, not ectropion uveae |\n| **With ectropion of the lower lid** | ⚠️ The joke option, and it gives the whole trap away: it exists only because the two conditions share the word *ectropion*. One is a lid turning out; the other is iris pigment turning out. They have nothing to do with each other |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The sign matters because it means **ischaemia somewhere behind it** — most often an ischaemic CRVO or proliferative diabetic retinopathy, both taught in `L19.2`. Seeing pigment on the front of the iris should send the examiner to the fundus and to gonioscopy, not to the lid.\n\n⚠️ *Filed under Glaucoma. The bank prints this question inside its Eyelid topic, which is a misfiling on the bank's part; the only lid content in it is the distractor (secondary chapter: Lid).*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Ectropion uveae is eversion of the iris pigment epithelium onto the anterior iris by a contracting rubeotic membrane, and it accompanies neovascular glaucoma — not uveitis, pupil-block angle closure, or any lid disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.27 (Q191), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-192', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'Concerning the **lid**:',
    options:['The medial canthus is acute & measures 60 degrees when the eyes are open.','When the eye is looking ahead, the lid covers 1-2 mm of the upper limbus.','The posterior lid margin is rounded.','The white line is the line through which the lid could be split into anterior & posterior lamellae.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Option B is the ptosis definition read the other way round.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 75 defines ptosis as \"Drooping of the upper eyelid below its normal position, so that it covers **more than 2 mm of the superior limbus** in the primary position\" — and \"more than 2 mm\" is highlighted yellow in the original. If more than 2 mm is abnormal, then **1–2 mm of upper limbal cover in primary gaze is normal**, which is exactly what B states.\n\nThe complementary rule for the **upper** boundary is on printed 76, in the retraction section: \"The **sclera above the limbus should not be visible** in the resting position of the upper eyelid. Lid retraction causes the sclera to be visible ('**scleral show**').\" So the normal upper lid sits in a narrow band: covering 1–2 mm of cornea, never showing sclera above it.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and each is a genuine anatomical fact with one word switched:**\n\n| Option | What is wrong | What the book actually says |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Medial canthus acute, 60°** | The **medial** canthus is **rounded** — it has to be, because it houses the lacus lacrimalis, the caruncle and the two puncta. It is the **lateral** canthus that is the acute angle, and it lies in contact with the globe | The book gives neither an angle nor a shape for either canthus; it says only that \"The upper and the lower lids in each eye join at the medial and lateral canthi\" (printed 71). *The rounded-medial / acute-lateral contrast and the 60° figure are not taken from the course material* |\n| **Posterior lid margin is rounded** | Exactly inverted. Printed 71: \"The lashes are present on the **rounded anterior border** of the lid margin … **The posterior lid margin is right-angled** and is in close coaptation with the globe\" | The right angle is functional: it is what lets the margin seal against the globe and spread the tear film |\n| **White line splits the lid** | Wrong line. Printed 71: the **white line** is \"The virtual line connecting these [meibomian gland] orifices\". The line that splits the lid is the **grey line** — \"an **avascular line** marking the separation of the lid **surgically** into two leaflets\" | ⚠️ This is the topic's sharpest trap and the corpus already tests the same landmark at `opmcq-c1-31`, keyed \"It marks the separation of the lid into anterior and posterior leaflets\", and its surgical use at `opmcq-c1-1` |\n\n**The two lid-margin landmarks, side by side, because this question and three others in the corpus turn on them:**\n\n| Line | What it is | What it is for |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Grey line** | Avascular line **in front of** the meibomian orifices, at the junction of the two lamellae | The **surgical splitting plane**: anterior leaflet = skin + subcutaneous tissue + orbicularis; posterior leaflet = tarsus + palpebral conjunctiva. The first suture in a lid laceration goes here (`opqb-t5-195`), and the trichiasis graft goes into a groove cut along it (book printed 78) |\n| **White line** | The line joining the **meibomian gland orifices** on the posterior margin | A landmark only — it marks where meibum reaches the tear film |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The lid margin has a front and a back and they behave differently: rounded anterior border with the lashes; right-angled posterior border with the meibomian orifices, sealing against the globe. Every lid lesion in this topic can be placed on one side of the grey line or the other, and that placement decides how it is drained (`opqb-t5-180`, `opqb-t5-188`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The normal upper lid covers 1–2 mm of the superior limbus; the posterior lid margin is right-angled, the medial canthus rounded, and it is the grey line — not the white line — that splits the lid into its two lamellae.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.27 (Q192), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-193', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A **50-year-old male** patient presented with a **3 mm well circumscribed painless swelling of the upper lid**; it was **firm, adherent to the tarsus but not to overlying skin**:',
    options:['It should be removed through a conjunctival incision & curettage.','Repeated recurrences of the swelling should be treated by intralesional steroids.','If it enlarges it may induce a low degree of myopia.','If pain develops with yellow pointing on conjunctival side, it should be evacuated by incision & curettage.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Every clause of the stem is a chalazion sign, and the operation follows from the one that matters: *adherent to the tarsus, not to the skin*.** The lesion is inside the posterior lamella, so it is reached from the posterior lamella. `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 73, under the chalazion's surgical treatment: \"**Incision and curettage through the conjunctival side**\" — and the cache notes this is the whole of the operative detail the chapter prints, with no incision direction, no clamp and no anaesthetic named.\n\n**Reading the stem against the book's own signs, printed 73:**\n\n| Stem | Book |\n|---|---|\n| painless | \"Gradually enlarging **painless** rounded nodule\" |\n| firm, well circumscribed | \"A **firm nodule within the tarsal plate**\" |\n| adherent to tarsus, not to skin | The lesion is *in* the tarsus — the posterior leaflet — so the skin slides freely over it. A lesion tethered to **skin** would be an anterior-lamella lesion, and a **fixed, ulcerated** one in a 50-year-old would raise carcinoma |\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` slide 19, headed \"Chalazion / Excision\", photographs the operation without a word: a **chalazion clamp** applied to the **everted** lid, then the everted lid inside the clamp ring after incision \"showing the yellow-white gelatinous chalazion contents being expressed/curetted from within the tarsus\". Everting the lid *is* the conjunctival approach.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and each is a rule from a neighbouring question:**\n\n**B — repeated recurrences do NOT go on getting steroid; they go to the laboratory.** Book printed 73: \"A **recurrent chalazion** (especially in elderly individuals) should be **biopsied** to exclude an underlying malignancy (**meibomian gland carcinoma**).\" That is the chapter's only oncological sentence, and `opqb-t5-222` keys exactly it — excision **and histopathology** for a chalazion that has recurred three times. Intralesional steroid is a legitimate treatment for a *first* chalazion (the book lists it immediately above incision and curettage); repeating it into a recurrence is how a sebaceous or meibomian gland carcinoma gets treated as a cyst for two years.\n\n**C — the induced error is ASTIGMATISM, not myopia.** A tarsal nodule presses on the cornea and **flattens it in one meridian**, so the refractive change is astigmatic and meridional, not a uniform increase in axial power. `opqb-t5-180` keys the visual consequence itself. *(The astigmatic mechanism is not taken from the course material — the book's chalazion entry prints no visual consequence at all.)*\n\n**D — a painful lesion pointing yellow on the conjunctival side is an acutely infected one, and that is the situation in which it must NOT be cut.** Book printed 72, hordeolum internum: \"It should **NEVER** be incised during active inflammation **UNLESS** it forms an abscess\" (both words underlined in the original). The correct first move there is systemic and topical antibiotics with hot fomentation — which is what `opqb-t5-215` and `opqb-t5-218` key.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Quiet chalazion → incise and curette from the **conjunctival** side. Infected chalazion → antibiotics first, drain only if it abscesses. **Recurrent** chalazion → excise **and send it**.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A quiet chalazion is removed by incision and curettage through the conjunctival side; recurrence demands biopsy, a large one induces astigmatism, and an acutely infected one is not incised.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.27 (Q193), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-194', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A **60-year-old male** patient presented with **severe bilateral ptosis of 6 months** duration; the **levator function was 15 mm**:',
    options:['The upper lid creases are about 8 mm from lid margin','The frontalis suspension should be performed','Diplopia may be associated due to weak convergence','This condition may be induced by cataract surgery'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"This is probably a case of **aponeurosis** due to stretch & injury to the levator muscle aponeurosis by the **speculum that keeps the lids open during cataract surgery,** a very rare post-operative complication\n\n---\n\n*(The paragraph above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The single number in the stem settles the diagnosis.** Levator function of **15 mm is normal** — see `opqb-t5-190`. A severe ptosis with a *normally functioning muscle* cannot be a muscle problem; the force is being generated and not delivered. That is **aponeurotic ptosis**: the levator aponeurosis has stretched, thinned or pulled off the tarsus. The book's aetiology 5, printed 76: \"**Aponeurotic (Senile):** Due to **disinsertion or dehiscence of the levator aponeurosis**. Associated with **advancing age, trauma, or ocular surgery**. Typically shows a **good levator function**.\"\n\n**Why cataract surgery does it, which is what the printed box is telling you.** The lid speculum holds the lids apart under tension for the length of the operation, and a superior rectus bridle suture — where one is used — pulls in the same direction. In an elderly lid whose aponeurosis is already thinned, that sustained stretch is enough to dehisce it. The ptosis appears in the weeks after surgery and is bilateral if both eyes have been operated, which fits the stem exactly: 60 years old, **bilateral**, **6 months**, normal function.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Creases about 8 mm from the lid margin** | 8 mm is a **normal** crease height. In aponeurotic ptosis the crease is dragged **up**, often to 12 mm or more, because the aponeurosis that makes it has retracted — a **high** crease is one of the three signs keyed at `opqb-t5-184`. *(Crease measurements are not taken from the course material; the book prints none.)* |\n| **Frontalis suspension** | The sling is for **poor** levator function — `L5) Eyelid disease` slide 9, \"Severe ptosis with poor levator function ( **4 mm or less** )\". With 15 mm of function the operation is an **aponeurosis repair or levator resection**; slide 8 sets the threshold at \"at least 5 mm\". Doing a sling here would tether a perfectly good muscle to the brow |\n| **Diplopia due to weak convergence** | Aponeurotic ptosis is a **lid** problem — the extraocular muscles and the pupil are untouched. Ptosis *with* diplopia points elsewhere: a **third-nerve palsy** (`opqb-t5-170`) or **myasthenia gravis**, where both the lid and the eye muscles fatigue and `L5` slide 13 photographs \"variability\", the **Ice Pack Test** and the \"**TensilonTest**\". Convergence weakness is not a mechanism of ptosis at all |\n\n⚠️ **Note on the printing.** The source sets \"ptosis of **6months** duration\", \"the levator function **was15mm**\", \"The **frontal is** suspension should be performed\" for *frontalis suspension*, and \"**Diploma** may be associated due to weak convergence\" for *Diplopia*. All four are repaired above; the printings are recorded here.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A new ptosis after cataract surgery with **good levator function and a high crease** is aponeurotic and is repaired by re-attaching the aponeurosis — not by a sling, and not by watching it.",
    objective:'Aponeurotic ptosis has good levator function and a high lid crease, and lid-speculum stretch at cataract surgery is a recognised cause; it is repaired at the aponeurosis, never by a frontalis sling.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.28 (Q194), key p.32 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-195', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'In **repair of a lacerated eyelid**, care should be taken for all of the following **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['The first suture should be in the grey line','Suture layer by layer','Instil antibiotics topically','Intubate the lacerated canaliculus'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Three of these are principles of lid *reconstruction*; the fourth is ordinary wound care that applies to any laceration anywhere and needs no special care in the lid.**\n\n| Option | Why it needs care |\n|---|---|\n| **First suture in the grey line** | ✅ The grey line is the visible surface landmark of the junction between the two lamellae. Putting the first suture there **aligns the margin** before anything else is closed; a millimetre of malalignment at the margin becomes a permanent notch, and a notch causes epiphora and lash malposition. The book defines the landmark on printed 71 — \"an avascular line marking the separation of the lid **surgically** into two leaflets\" |\n| **Suture layer by layer** | ✅ The lid is five layers (book printed 71: skin, subcutaneous tissue, orbicularis, tarsus, palpebral conjunctiva) and the two lamellae must be repaired separately. Closing skin over an unrepaired tarsus leaves a lid without a skeleton — it buckles, and cicatricial malposition follows |\n| **Intubate the lacerated canaliculus** | ✅ The single most consequential step, and the one most often missed. A **medial** lid laceration divides the canaliculus, and a divided canaliculus that is not stented over a silicone tube heals stenosed — permanent epiphora. This is why the *medial* end of a lid wound is examined before anything is sutured |\n| **Instil antibiotics topically** | ❌ **The exception.** Sensible, routine, and not a technical point of lid repair |\n\n⚠️ **Lid laceration repair is absent from both sources, and that was measured.** `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.5's own audit reads: \"Eyelid **TRAUMA and lid laceration repair — ABSENT as a section**. 'Lacerated wounds of the eyelids' appears once, only as a cause of **cicatricial ectropion** (printed 78). The book's trauma chapter is 18, **UNREAD**.\" `L5) Eyelid disease` names three operations in 47 slides — levator resection, frontalis brow suspension and chalazion excision — and \"graft\", \"flap\" and \"reconstruc\" all return zero across its typed text. So the four principles above are *not taken from the course material*; what **is** sourced is the grey line itself (book printed 71) and the five-layer anatomy it separates.\n\n**The two rules that make lid repair different from skin repair**, and both follow from the anatomy the book does print. First, **the margin is a functional edge**, not a cosmetic one: it seals the tear film against the globe, and the book insists on the right-angled posterior margin \"in close coaptation with the globe\" (printed 71). Second, **the medial third contains the drainage apparatus** — puncta, canaliculi, the medial canthal tendon — and it is the part of the lid most often torn, because a blow that pulls the lid laterally tears it at its weakest attachment.\n\n⚠️ **Note on the printing.** The source splits two verbs: option C reads \"**In still** antibiotics topically\" and option D \"**In tubate** the lacerated canaliculus\". Both are repaired above.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** In any lid laceration: **look medially first** (canaliculus), **align the grey line first** (margin), then close **layer by layer**. ⚠️ *Filed under Trauma; the bank prints it in its Eyelid topic (secondary chapter: Lid).*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Lid laceration repair turns on aligning the margin at the grey line, closing layer by layer, and stenting a divided canaliculus; topical antibiotics are routine wound care, not a special precaution.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.28 (Q195), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-196', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'All of the following are related to **senile entropion EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Treated by everting sutures','Lead to blindness','Lacrimation is the main symptom','Mainly in the lower lid'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Irritation & foreign body sensation is the main symptom**\n\n---\n\n*(The line above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The book prints the symptom list, and lacrimation is on it — but third.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 77: \"This results in the **lashes rubbing against the conjunctiva and cornea**, with **reflex lacrimation, foreign body sensation and red eye**.\" All three symptoms are real; the question asks which is the *main* one, and the bank's box answers it. The mechanism supports the box: the lashes are physically abrading the corneal epithelium, and corneal epithelial abrasion is felt as **grittiness and foreign body sensation** by the richest sensory innervation in the body. The tearing is *reflex* — a consequence of that irritation, not the primary complaint.\n\n**Why the other three are true and therefore not the exception:**\n\n| Option | Verdict | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mainly in the lower lid** | ✅ \"**Most commonly affects the lower lid**\", and the book adds that cicatricial entropion is the only type that also takes the upper lid | Book printed 77 |\n| **Lead to blindness** | ✅ \"May cause **corneal abrasions with secondary corneal scarring**. (If infectious keratitis occurs, it may lead to **loss of vision**)\" | Book printed 77. `opqb-t5-197` keys the same consequence for the cicatricial type |\n| **Treated by everting sutures** | ✅ Everting sutures rotate the margin outward by re-attaching the lax lower-lid retractors. ⚠️ The book names **no** entropion operation — its treatment reads only \"Surgical treatment. Topical medications to control ocular surface irritation and inflammation (mainly lubricants)\", and its own audit records that \"NAMED entropion or ectropion OPERATIONS — ABSENT\". `L5) Eyelid disease` names no operation for either malposition across its six entropion/ectropion slides. So the *name* of the procedure is *not taken from the course material*; the mechanism it corrects — \"laxity of the **lower lid retractors** and lateral canthal ligament\" — is the book's own, printed 77 |\n\n**Why the symptom question is worth asking at all.** It separates the three lid malpositions by what the patient actually complains of:\n\n| Malposition | Chief complaint | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Entropion** | **Foreign body sensation, grittiness, red eye** | Lashes abrading the cornea |\n| **Ectropion** | **Epiphora** — watering | \"The eyelids cannot effectively push tears toward the tear drain\" (book printed 77), and the punctum is turned away from the tear lake |\n| **Lagophthalmos** | Dryness, then pain, then loss of vision | Exposure of the lower cornea |\n\nSo *watering* is the presenting symptom of **ectropion**, and offering it as the main symptom of entropion is the swap the question is built on. `opqb-t1-17` in the shipped corpus is exactly that ectropion presentation — a 78-year-old complaining of excessive tearing.\n\n⚠️ **Near-repeat.** `opqb-t5-176` is also \"senile entropion … EXCEPT\", with two options overlapping this one; it is nevertheless a different question with a different exception (the Snellen operation).",
    objective:'Senile entropion presents with foreign body sensation from lashes abrading the cornea, not with lacrimation; watering is the presenting symptom of ectropion.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.28 (Q196), key p.32 (explanation box printed)' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-197', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Cicatricial entropion**:',
    options:["Mainly treated by Webster's operation","Snellen's operation for treating lower lid","It's a blinding disease",'It will cause corneal opacification & blindness'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Cicatricial entropion is the blinding one, and the reason is that it is the type that takes the UPPER lid.** The book states the exception on printed 77: \"Unlike other causes of entropion that affect **lower lids only**, this type **may affect both upper and lower lids**\" — and the cache calls that sentence the chapter's most repeated discriminator, printed again for ectropion on printed 78. An inturned **upper** lid drags a full row of lashes across the **central** cornea with every blink, thousands of times a day, and the visual axis is what scars. A lower-lid entropion abrades the inferior cornea, below the axis.\n\nThe book supplies the pathway to blindness in the general entropion entry, printed 77: \"May cause **corneal abrasions with secondary corneal scarring**. (**If infectious keratitis occurs, it may lead to loss of vision**).\" Chronic epithelial breakdown is an open door for bacterial keratitis, and a healed central scar is a permanent opacity.\n\n**And the classic cause makes the point.** The book's list of what shortens the conjunctiva, printed 77: \"Trauma · Chronic inflammation (Autoimmune ocular surface diseases e.g., **Stevens-Johnson syndrome**) · Infection (**trachoma**)\". Trachoma is the world's leading infectious cause of blindness, and it blinds precisely by this route — conjunctival scarring, upper-lid entropion, trichiasis, corneal opacification. `L5) Eyelid disease` slide 28, headed \"Entropion / Cicatricial\", photographs both lids inturned in two panels and prints no other word.\n\n⚠️ **KEY WORTH FLAGGING, and it is recorded, not disputed.** Options **C** and **D** say the same thing by different routes — D states the mechanism (\"corneal opacification\") of which C states the outcome (\"a blinding disease\") — and both read as true against book printed 77. The bank keys **C**, and C stands as the `answer` here. A candidate who chose D has understood the disease.\n\n**Why A and B are wrong:**\n\n⚠️ **Neither Webster's nor Snellen's operation appears anywhere in the course material** — both names were grepped case-insensitively across every file in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and `content\\ophtho\\book\\`. `Webster` returns **zero hits in the entire module**; every `Snellen` hit is the **visual-acuity chart** (`L1,2`, `L3,4`, `L7,8`, `L37`, book ch.1). What can be reconstructed comes from the bank's own keys, and only that far: `opqb-t5-176` keys \"Can be treated by Snellen\" as **false for the senile type**, and this question keys \"Snellen's operation for treating **lower** lid\" as **false** — so the bank places Snellen's operation on the **upper** lid, i.e. on cicatricial entropion, the only type that takes it. `opqb-t5-179` keys Webster as **not** a trichiasis treatment, and this question keys it as **not** the main treatment here. *(That reconstruction is inference from the bank's keys, not from the course material.)*\n\n**What the treatment principle actually is**, whatever the eponyms: cicatricial entropion is a problem of **posterior-lamella shortage**, so the repair must **lengthen or rotate** — tarsal fracture and rotation, or a mucous-membrane or hard-palate graft to replace the missing conjunctiva. Contrast senile entropion, a problem of **laxity**, where the repair **tightens** (`opqb-t5-176`, `opqb-t5-196`). Applying a tightening operation to a shortage does nothing at all. *(Not taken from the course material — the book's entropion treatment is the two words \"Surgical treatment.\")*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Cicatricial entropion is the only type that takes the upper lid, so it scars the central cornea and blinds; it is a posterior-lamella shortage needing a lengthening or rotating operation, not a tightening one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.28 (Q197), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-198', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'The photograph shows one eye of an elderly, pale-skinned patient in close-up — the brow above, the cheek below, a blue-grey iris behind a clear cornea. **The lower lid and its margin fill the lower half of the frame; the inferior bulbar conjunctiva is injected and the lid skin below is lax and crinkled, with a pale keratinised streak running along the margin.** What is the name of the following condition?',
    options:['Entropion','Ectropion','Chalazion','None of the above'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ THIS QUESTION HAS A FIGURE, and the bank's printed stem is only \"What is the name of the following condition?\" — it names no finding at all, so it cannot be answered without the photograph. The stem above restates the general scene so the entry does not stand empty, and deliberately stops short of the one thing that answers the question: **the position of the lower lid margin relative to the globe**, which has to be read off the image. The crop does not exist yet; its column, book page and 400 dpi pixel bounds are recorded in `gg-t5.array.js`, and the photograph carries no printed text, letter or caption inside it.)*\n\n**What the printed key means, and how the two candidate answers are told apart.** The book defines both malpositions in one line each and they are exact opposites:\n\n| | Entropion — book printed 77 | Ectropion — book printed 77 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Margin | \"**rotated inwards toward the eye**\" | \"**rotated outward away from the eye**\" |\n| What you see at the margin | The **lash line disappears from view** — the lashes have been carried back against the globe | A **red band of palpebral conjunctiva** is exposed along the lid, and the lashes point down and away |\n| Symptom | \"reflex lacrimation, **foreign body sensation** and red eye\" | \"**epiphora**\" — the punctum is turned away and cannot reach the tear lake |\n| Cornea | Abraded by lashes; may scar | Dried by exposure |\n\n**`L5) Eyelid disease` photographs both, in consecutive slides, with headings and no other word.** Slide 29, \"Entropion / Senile (Involutional)\": \"The LOWER LID MARGIN IS ROLLED INWARD along its whole length: **the lash line has disappeared from view** and the lashes turn back and up against the globe. The lower lid skin below is loose, pink and crinkled into a horizontal fold.\" Slide 32, \"Ectropion / Senile (Involutional)\": \"The LOWER LID IS TURNED OUTWARD along its whole length, showing **a red strip of palpebral conjunctiva** below the globe\", and in its second panel the exposed tarsal conjunctiva has become \"THICKENED AND WHITISH — **a keratinised band**\".\n\n**So the sign to look for is the lash line.** If you can count the lower lashes standing out from the margin, the lid has not turned in. If the lash row has vanished from view and the margin sits hard against the globe, it has.\n\n**Why chalazion and \"none of the above\" are wrong.** A chalazion is a discrete **rounded nodule within the tarsal plate** away from the margin (book printed 73) — a lump, not a malposition, and there is no lump here. \"None of the above\" fails because the key names one of the first three.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Both senile malpositions are diseases of lower-lid **laxity** in the elderly and both are photographed here from a distance at which the diagnosis is a single feature: *inward, lashes hidden* versus *outward, conjunctiva showing*. Get that one feature and the treatment follows — tighten and rotate out for entropion, tighten and rotate in for ectropion.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Entropion is inward rotation of the lid margin with the lash line hidden and the lashes against the globe; ectropion is outward rotation with the palpebral conjunctiva exposed.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.28 (Q198, figure printed in the right column), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-199', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A **4-year-old child** is brought by his parents because they noticed that his eyes "look crossed" inwards. On examination, **the corneal light reflex is central and symmetrical.** What is the most likely diagnosis?',
    options:['Esotropia','Epicanthus fold','Entropion','Ptosis'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A central, symmetrical corneal light reflex means the eyes are straight — so whatever the parents are seeing is not a squint.** This is **pseudoesotropia**, and in a small child with a broad flat nasal bridge the cause is an **epicanthal fold**.\n\n**The book states the illusion and its mechanism.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 79: \"Epicanthal folds are **bilateral vertical folds of skin** that extend from the upper or lower lids towards the **medial canthi**, hiding the medial canthal tendon behind them (hence the name epicanthus). They may result in **giving the false appearance of having a convergent squint (pseudoesotropia)**.\"\n\n**Why the fold produces the illusion.** The fold covers the **medial** sclera of each eye. The observer judges eye alignment by comparing the width of white sclera on either side of the cornea; hide the nasal white and each eye looks as if it has turned in towards the nose. The illusion is strongest on side gaze, when the adducting eye's nasal sclera disappears entirely behind the fold. It resolves as the nasal bridge grows.\n\n**Why the corneal reflex is the whole examination.** A light held in front of the child reflects off both corneas. If the eyes are **straight**, both reflexes sit **centrally and symmetrically** — the Hirschberg test. If one eye is **esotropic**, its reflex is displaced **temporally** on that cornea, because the cornea has rotated nasally under it. There is no way for a genuine esotropia to give symmetrical central reflexes.\n\n⚠️ `L5) Eyelid disease` slide 4 is headed \"Epicanthus\" and photographs it three times with no body text at all: an infant \"with a broad flat nasal bridge and bilateral vertical skin folds running from the upper lid down over the medial canthi, **covering the medial sclera and giving the appearance of esotropia** (pseudostrabismus). **Both corneal light reflexes are central.**\" The book's Fig (5-11) shows the same, and its cache flags that the central reflexes — \"the finding that proves the squint is false\" — are visible in the photograph and **never mentioned in the prose**.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Esotropia** | Excluded by the examination, not by the history. A real convergent squint displaces the reflex temporally in the deviating eye, and would need cover testing, cycloplegic refraction and treatment for amblyopia |\n| **Entropion** | A lid-margin malposition causing a red, gritty eye. It does not alter apparent eye position and would not be described as \"looking crossed\" |\n| **Ptosis** | A drooping upper lid. It narrows a fissure; it does not make an eye look turned in |\n\n**Clinical bottom line — and it is the reason the question exists.** Pseudoesotropia is reassured, **but it does not protect the child from also having a real squint.** Any child brought because the eyes look crossed gets a corneal reflex test and a cover test, and if there is any doubt, a cycloplegic refraction. ⚠️ Q200 asks what the *treatment* of this condition is for, and it follows directly.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Central symmetrical corneal light reflexes exclude a true squint; bilateral epicanthal folds hiding the medial sclera produce pseudoesotropia in a young child.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.28 (Q199), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-200', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'In the previous question — the 4-year-old whose apparently crossed eyes were shown by a central, symmetrical corneal light reflex to be due to **bilateral epicanthal folds** — the main reason for treatment in this condition is:',
    options:['To restore vision','To relieve eye pain','Cosmetic reason','To correct corneal exposure'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ BACK-REFERENCE. The bank's printed stem is \"In the previous Q, the main reason for treatment in this condition is:\". The antecedent is the immediately preceding question — the epicanthus vignette at `opqb-t5-199` — and it has been restated above so this entry stands alone.)*\n\n**The book gives the indication in one sentence, and it is the whole answer.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 79, last line of the epicanthus entry: \"**Treatment for cosmetic reasons is surgical.**\"\n\n**Why there is no other reason to treat.** An epicanthal fold is a **skin fold**. It does not touch the globe, does not obstruct the visual axis, does not alter refraction, and does not interfere with lid closure. The eyes underneath it are **straight** — that is what the central symmetrical corneal reflexes established at `opqb-t5-199`. So:\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **To restore vision** | ❌ Vision is normal. There is no deprivation (the axis is clear), no anisometropia and no strabismic suppression, because there is no strabismus. Contrast a **true** esotropia or a severe congenital **ptosis**, where the indication genuinely is amblyopia — the key at `opqb-t5-187` |\n| **To relieve eye pain** | ❌ The fold is painless. Pain in this topic belongs to the acute lesions — the stye (`opqb-t5-210`), the infected chalazion (`opqb-t5-215`) — and to the abraded cornea of entropion (`opqb-t5-196`) |\n| **To correct corneal exposure** | ❌ Exposure is a **lagophthalmos** problem: lids that fail to close, cornea drying and ulcerating inferiorly (`opqb-t5-201`, `opqb-t5-202`, `opqb-t5-216`). An epicanthal fold covers the medial *canthus*, never the cornea |\n| **Cosmetic reason** | ✅ The book's own words |\n\n**A practical point the book does not print.** The fold usually **becomes less obvious as the nasal bridge develops**, so surgery — a medial canthoplasty or Y-to-V type skin procedure — is generally deferred, and is offered for appearance rather than function. Operating early on a fold that will resolve buys a scar for nothing. *(Not taken from the course material — the book prints only the one sentence above.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Two things follow from \"the reason is cosmetic\". First, the parents' real need is **reassurance plus a proper squint assessment**, not an operation. Second, this is one of the few conditions in the whole eyelid topic where doing nothing is a complete answer — which is exactly why the bank asks it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Epicanthus is treated for appearance only — vision, comfort and corneal cover are all normal because the eyes beneath the folds are straight.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.28 (Q200), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-201', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A **45-year-old male** presents with **inability to close his left eye completely** after a recent episode of **facial weakness**. The cornea appears **dry and mildly ulcerated inferiorly**. The most likely cause of this presentation is:',
    options:['Oculomotor nerve palsy','Trigeminal nerve palsy','Facial nerve palsy','Trochlear nerve palsy'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The eye will not close, so the muscle that closes it has failed — and orbicularis oculi is supplied by the facial nerve.** Book printed 71: \"Muscular layer containing fibers of the **orbicularis oculi**, the muscle responsible for **blinking and forcible closure** of the eyelids. It is supplied by the **facial (VII) nerve**.\"\n\nThe book's lagophthalmos aetiology puts it first, printed 80: \"**1. Facial nerve dysfunction (paralytic lagophthalmos):** It leads to **weakened eyelid closure**. Facial nerve can be damaged by: a) Trauma … b) Cerebrovascular accident c) **Bell's palsy** … d) Tumours in the cerebellopontine angle.\" The stem's \"recent episode of facial weakness\" is Bell's palsy in everything but name.\n\n**Why the ulcer is inferior, and why that detail is the confirmation rather than the decoration.** Lids that do not meet leave the **lower third** of the cornea uncovered. That strip dries first, the epithelium breaks down, and **exposure keratopathy** becomes an **exposure ulcer**. The book: \"The ocular surface become subject to damage from **exposure**\" (printed 80). A dry, inferiorly ulcerated cornea in a patient who cannot close the eye is the complete picture.\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` slide 15, headed \"Lagophthalmos / Paralytic\", photographs the same patient type: an elderly woman attempting to close both eyes, the right closing fully, \"on the LEFT the lids remain apart with the globe **rolled up** so only white sclera and the lower cornea show\", and the left side of the face paralysed — \"the nasolabial fold is flattened and the mouth is pulled to the right\". ⚠️ The deck names nothing: \"Bell\" returns **zero** across all 170 of its typed words, so neither Bell's palsy nor Bell's phenomenon is printed on the slide that shows both.\n\n**Bell's phenomenon is why some of these eyes survive.** Book printed 80: \"the eyeball (and thus the cornea) **rotates upward and inwards upon eyelid closure**, particularly during sleep, [and] may serve to **protect the cornea**\". A patient with a good Bell's phenomenon presents with irritation; one with a poor Bell's presents with an ulcer.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it supplies | What its palsy gives |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Oculomotor (III)** | Levator, four extraocular muscles, pupil sphincter | **Ptosis** with a down-and-out eye and a dilated pupil — `opqb-t5-170`. The lid closes perfectly; it is the *opening* that fails |\n| **Trigeminal (V)** | **Sensation** to the cornea; motor to mastication | Lid closure is **normal**. What is lost is corneal sensation, giving **neurotrophic** keratopathy — a cornea that ulcerates painlessly and presents late. The shipped `opqb-t3-115` works this exact comparison in a table |\n| **Trochlear (IV)** | Superior oblique alone | Vertical/torsional diplopia and a head tilt. No lid function whatever |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** ⚠️ Both nerves can end in a scarred cornea and the routes are opposite: **VII → lids do not close → exposure keratopathy, painful**; **V → lids close fine → neurotrophic keratopathy, painless**. If a patient with a facial palsy also has an **anaesthetic** cornea, both are involved and the eye is in far more danger than either lesion alone. Q202 asks what to do about this eye today.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A facial (VII) nerve palsy paralyses orbicularis, producing paralytic lagophthalmos and inferior exposure keratopathy; III causes ptosis, V causes neurotrophic keratopathy with normal closure.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.28 (Q201), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-202', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'In the previous question — the **45-year-old with paralytic lagophthalmos after facial weakness**, whose cornea is dry and mildly ulcerated inferiorly — what is the **best immediate management to protect the cornea**?',
    options:['Topical steroids','Artificial tears and eyelid taping','Warm compress','Surgical correction only'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ BACK-REFERENCE. The bank's printed stem is \"In the previous Q, what is the best immediate management to protect the cornea?\". The antecedent is the immediately preceding question, `opqb-t5-201`, and it is restated above so this entry stands alone. Note that Q201 is the last question on book p.25's run-out page 28 and Q202 opens p.29 — the antecedent is on the previous sheet.)*\n\n**The book's treatment list opens with exactly these two measures, and it says what they are for.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 81: \"**Treatment: (Aim is to protect the cornea)** · **Prophylactic extensive lubrication with artificial tears** (preferably with preservative-free eye drops especially in cases that are expected to last for a long time) · Long-acting agents such as **artificial tear gel and ointments at bedtime** · Treatment of associated corneal exposure or ulceration. · **Overnight tapping of the lids** to close the eye. · Tarsorrhaphy … · Gold weight implantation in the upper lid.\"\n\nThe chapter's tutorial box on the same page splits it into stages, which is the framework the question is testing: \"**A. Temporary measures:** • Artificial tears • At bedtime: ointment and **tapping of the lids by strips of adhesive plaster**. **B. Permanent measures:** o Lateral tarsorrhaphy & medial canthoplasty. **C. Treatment of the cause**.\"\n\n**Why the answer is the temporary pair and not the permanent one.** The stem asks for the **immediate** management. Bell's palsy recovers in most cases, so the eye needs to be carried through the weeks of exposure without being committed to an operation that narrows the fissure permanently. Lubricants replace the tear film the failed blink is not spreading; **taping** physically closes the lids overnight, when the eye is unprotected for eight hours at a stretch and when exposure ulcers actually happen.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Topical steroids** | ⚠️ **Actively dangerous here.** There is already an **ulcerated** cornea. Steroid impairs epithelial healing, promotes stromal melting through collagenase activity, and masks and worsens any infection that gets into that ulcer. It treats nothing about exposure |\n| **Warm compress** | The treatment of **hordeolum and chalazion** — book printed 72 and 73, \"Warm compresses with gentle massage\". It has no role in exposure; warming a drying cornea does not wet it |\n| **Surgical correction only** | The word **only** is what kills it. Tarsorrhaphy and gold weight implantation are the book's own measures (printed 81, and `opqb-t5-206` keys the gold weight), but they are for **persistent** lagophthalmos after recovery has failed. Going straight to surgery in the first weeks of a Bell's palsy commits a lid that may recover |\n\n**Clinical bottom line — the escalation, in order.** **Lubricate → ointment and tape at night → gold weight or tarsorrhaphy if it does not recover.** And treat the ulcer that is already there: `opqb-t5-216` keys exposure corneal ulcer as the complication to guard against in the same clinical situation.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Immediate protection in paralytic lagophthalmos is lubrication plus overnight lid taping; steroids endanger an ulcerated cornea and surgery is reserved for lagophthalmos that does not recover.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.29 (Q202), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-203', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A **52-year-old woman** presents with **yellowish plaques on the medial aspect of both upper eyelids**. What is the best management for **cosmetic removal**?',
    options:['Topical steroids','Surgical excision','Cryotherapy','Antibiotic ointment'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Bilateral yellow plaques at the medial ends of the upper lids in a woman in her fifties is xanthelasma, and the book gives three treatments — of which surgical excision is the one that removes the lesion.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 80: \"**Treatment:** ▪ **Conservative therapy** low-fat diet and statins. - **The therapeutic effect is questionable.** ▪ **Surgical excision.** ▪ CO2 laser therapy **→ Tutorial**.\"\n\nThe stem's every detail is the book's own description, printed 79 and 80: \"a **yellowish-white lesion** found on the **medial aspect** of the skin of the eyelids\"; \"It occurs more in **middle-aged individuals (4th to 5th decades of life)**\"; \"soft, yellow plaques filled with cholesterol at the medial canthus\"; \"multiple, **symmetric** lesions that more commonly are found on the **upper eyelid** than the lower one\"; and — the sentence that makes this question's framing the book's own — \"**Patients seek treatment for cosmetic concerns.** Diagnosis is clinical.\"\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` slide 34 is headed \"Xanthelasma\" and prints nothing else; its photograph shows \"Flat-topped, soft, well-demarcated YELLOW-CREAM PLAQUES … at the **MEDIAL ends** of the lids on **BOTH SIDES**\", the plaques \"IN THE SKIN, well away from the lash line\", with the lid margins normal. The deck names no cause, no blood test and no operation. `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 6, \"Eyelid: you may find\", carries it as one of four labelled photographs — Ptosis, Lagophthalmos, BCC, Xanthelasma — again as a label only.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Topical steroids** | Xanthelasma is not inflammatory. It is a **deposit of cholesterol-laden foam cells in the dermis**, and no anti-inflammatory will clear it |\n| **Cryotherapy** | Freezing has a place in this topic — for **trichiasis**, where it kills lash follicles (`opqb-t5-179`, `opqb-t5-223`) — but the lid skin here is thin and cryotherapy of a plaque risks depigmentation, scarring and cicatricial lid malposition. The book does not list it |\n| **Antibiotic ointment** | There is no infection. Not a lesion, not a treatment |\n\n**The one thing to do besides removing it.** The book is emphatic that the lesion is a **marker**: \"Patients with xanthelasma usually have associated **lipid disorders** e.g., hyperlipidemia, high total cholesterol, high triglycerides, and low levels of high-density lipoprotein\", although \"many patients are **normolipidemic** on presentation\". Its risk factors are \"1. Diabetes Mellitus 2. Hypothyroidism 3. Cardiovascular disease 4. Obesity 5. heavy smoking\", and its investigations are \"Total lipid profile · HbA1C · Liver function tests · Thyroid function tests\" (printed 79–80). The book's systemic chapter makes the same link from the other end — ch.19, printed 243, lists **styes and xanthelasma** among the \"Other effects of DM on the eye\". So the plaque is excised for appearance **and the patient's lipids, glucose and thyroid are checked**.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Excision is definitive but the lesion **recurs**, especially where the underlying lipid abnormality is untreated — which is the practical argument for doing the blood tests rather than only the operation *(recurrence is not taken from the course material)*. `opqb-t5-207` asks the site.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Xanthelasma is removed surgically for cosmesis; it is a dermal cholesterol deposit, not an inflammatory or infective lesion, and it should prompt lipid, glucose and thyroid testing.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.29 (Q203), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-204', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'Which of the following complications may occur in **chronic staphylococcal blepharitis**?',
    options:['Madarosis','Pterygium','Papilledema','Cataract'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The book prints the complication list and madarosis heads it.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 74, staphylococcal (ulcerative) blepharitis: \"Lashes fall with scarring and notching of the lid margin leading to **madarosis (permanent loss of the lashes)**, trichiasis and poliosis (premature whitening of the lashes)\", and then the list proper — \"o Madarosis, trichiasis and poliosis. o Recurrent styes. o Chronic conjunctivitis, and phlyctens.\"\n\n**The mechanism is one chain.** *Staphylococcus aureus* and *S. epidermidis* colonise the lash bases; the margin ulcerates; ulceration heals with **scarring and notching**; scarring destroys **lash follicles**. Follicles that are destroyed give **madarosis**, follicles that survive distorted give **trichiasis**, follicles that survive depigmented give **poliosis**. All three of the lash complications, and the recurrent styes, come out of the same ulcerated margin.\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` gives madarosis its own slide — slide 24, one photograph, heading and nothing else: \"**BOTH LID MARGINS ARE COMPLETELY BALD**: not a single lash remains on the upper or the lower lid\", the margins now quiet, with \"no crust, no scale and no ulceration\". The deck names no cause of lash loss anywhere. The book's own complications atlas on printed 74 photographs four of them in a row — \"Madarosis\", \"Trichiasis\", \"Stye(recurrent )\" and \"Conjunctivitis & marginal keratitis\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and all three are real eye diseases in the wrong compartment:**\n\n| Option | What it is | Why not a blepharitis complication |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Pterygium** | A fibrovascular **conjunctival** encroachment onto the cornea, driven by ultraviolet exposure. Book ch.6 teaches it, and its own list of what causes it is environmental | Blepharitis does not produce it. Chronic conjunctivitis and marginal keratitis are the conjunctival and corneal complications the book *does* name |\n| **Papilloedema** | **Optic disc swelling from raised intracranial pressure** — a neuro-ophthalmic sign, an entire compartment away from the lid margin | Nothing on the lid margin raises intracranial pressure |\n| **Cataract** | Lens opacity | Not caused by lid disease. Note that *treatment* can be relevant elsewhere — long-term topical steroid causes cataract and glaucoma — but that is not what the option says |\n\n**The one complication worth knowing that the option list omits.** `ophthalmology.pdf` prints \"Chronic conjunctivitis, and phlyctens\" in the words, and its figure caption on printed 74 adds \"Conjunctivitis & **marginal keratitis**\" — a peripheral corneal infiltrate driven by hypersensitivity to staphylococcal exotoxin. The cache flags that **marginal keratitis appears in the whole chapter only in that caption**, so it is figure-only teaching: it is why chronic blepharitis can present as a painful red eye with a white peripheral corneal spot, and why treating the eye without treating the lid margin fails.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** The chain runs **margin ulceration → lash loss and misdirection → recurrent styes → chronic conjunctivitis and marginal keratitis**, and the treatment is at the top of it: lid hygiene, topical and where necessary systemic antibiotics (`opqb-t5-205`), and treating the margin to stop recurrence (`opqb-t5-219`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Chronic staphylococcal blepharitis scars the lid margin and destroys lash follicles, causing madarosis with trichiasis, poliosis, recurrent styes and chronic conjunctivitis.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.29 (Q204), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-205', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A **35-year-old man** presents with **chronic red, itchy eyelids, crusts around the lashes, and occasional loss of lashes**. Examination shows **greasy scales on lid margins and telangiectatic vessels**. The **first-line management** includes:',
    options:['Systemic steroids','Lid hygiene and warm compresses','Surgical excision','Antifungal eye drops'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Whichever blepharitis this is, the first line is the same, and the book prints it three separate times.** Printed 73, seborrhoeic anterior blepharitis: \"1. **Lid hygiene (warm compresses and lid scrubs with diluted baby shampoo)** 2. treatment of associated scalp seborrhoea.\" Printed 74, staphylococcal: \"– **Lid hygiene (warm compresses and lid scrubs with diluted baby shampoo)** – Topical (fusidic acid &azithromycin) – Systemic antibiotics (tetracyclines doxycycline, azithromycin) – Managing complications.\" Printed 75, posterior blepharitis: \"• **Eyelid hygiene (warm compresses and lid scrubs with diluted baby shampoo)** • Oral Tetracyclines or Doxycycline • Tear substitutes…\"\n\n⚠️ **The vignette deliberately mixes the types, and that is the point.** \"Greasy scales\" is **seborrhoeic anterior** blepharitis (printed 73: \"greasy soft scales on the lashes. (no ulceration)\"); \"crusts around the lashes\" with \"occasional loss of lashes\" leans **staphylococcal anterior** (printed 74: collarettes, madarosis); \"telangiectatic vessels\" is **posterior** blepharitis (printed 74: \"Hyperaemia and **telangiectasia of the posterior lid margin**\"). Mixed pictures are the rule in practice, and the first-line answer does not change.\n\n**Why lid hygiene works, mechanically.** Warm compresses **melt inspissated meibomian secretion** so it can be expressed and can reach the tear film again; lid scrubs **physically remove** the scales, collarettes and the bacterial load living in them. Nothing else in the option list addresses either.\n\n⚠️ **`L5) Eyelid disease` prints no lid-hygiene instruction at all** — measured on the deck's own text: \"hygiene\" appears once in the transcription and only as the slide-26 block recording its absence, and **no drug is named anywhere in the 47 slides**. Its three blepharitis slides (22, 23, 26) are photographs only. The management here comes entirely from the book.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Systemic steroids** | Wildly disproportionate for a chronic lid-margin condition, and blepharitis is driven by bacterial colonisation and glandular dysfunction, not by systemic inflammation. The book allows **topical** steroid only in posterior blepharitis, and then \"may be needed to control ocular surface inflammation\" — an adjunct, never first line |\n| **Surgical excision** | There is nothing to excise. Surgery in this topic is for chalazion, ptosis, malpositions and trichiasis |\n| **Antifungal eye drops** | No fungus. The named organisms are \"**S aureus and S epidermidis**\" (printed 74). ⚠️ **Demodex is not named as a cause of blepharitis anywhere in the book** and returns zero in `L5` — a real association in practice, but not one this material teaches |\n\n**What comes after hygiene, and it is worth having because the bank keys it elsewhere.** Topical fusidic acid or azithromycin for the staphylococcal form; **oral tetracycline or doxycycline** for posterior disease and for anterior disease that will not settle; tear substitutes for the associated dry eye; and — the rule of `opqb-t5-219` — treating the margin to prevent the recurrent styes and chalazia that brought the patient in.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Blepharitis is **controlled, not cured**. Lid hygiene is lifelong, and the commonest reason it \"fails\" is that it was stopped.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'First-line treatment for every type of blepharitis is lid hygiene — warm compresses and lid scrubs — with topical or oral antibiotics added according to type.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.29 (Q205), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-206', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Gold weight implantation** is used in management of:',
    options:['Ptosis','Lagophthalmos','Entropion','Blepharitis'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The book names it once, in one place, and it is the last line of the lagophthalmos treatment list.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 81: \"· Tarsorrhaphy (surgical suturing of the upper and lower lids to narrow the palpebral fissure and reduce exposure) · **Gold weight implantation in the upper lid.**\" ⚠️ Grepped across every file in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and `content\\ophtho\\book\\`, \"gold weight\" returns **that hit and no other** in the entire module.\n\n**Why a weight, and why in the upper lid.** In paralytic lagophthalmos the orbicularis cannot pull the upper lid down, but **gravity still works**. A thin gold plate sutured to the tarsus of the upper lid adds enough mass for the lid to fall closed when the levator relaxes — so the patient regains a **blink and lid closure in sleep** without any muscle. Gold is used because it is dense, inert and malleable, and because its colour is less conspicuous than the alternatives through thin lid skin. Crucially the implant **does not stop the lid opening**: the levator is intact in a VII palsy and simply lifts against the extra weight.\n\n**Why it beats the alternative in a recoverable palsy.** Tarsorrhaphy — the book's other named operation — closes the fissure by suturing the lids together, which protects the cornea but **narrows the visual field, is disfiguring, and has to be undone** if the facial nerve recovers. A gold weight is reversible by removing it and leaves the fissure looking normal. *(The comparison is not taken from the course material — the book prints both operations and no indication for choosing between them.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Its actual treatment | Why weight is wrong |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ptosis** | Levator resection if function ≥ 5 mm, frontalis sling if ≤ 4 mm (`L5` slides 8 and 9; `opqb-t5-169`, `opqb-t5-174`) | ⚠️ **Exactly backwards.** Ptosis is a lid that will not **rise**; adding weight to it makes it worse. Indeed the book's ptosis aetiology 6 is \"Mechanical: **Increased lid weight** from a lid mass\" (printed 76) — a gold weight in a ptotic lid would *be* the disease |\n| **Entropion** | Surgical rotation of the margin; everting sutures for the senile type | The problem is the margin's rotation, not the lid's height. Weight does not rotate a margin |\n| **Blepharitis** | Lid hygiene, topical and oral antibiotics (`opqb-t5-205`) | A medical condition of the margin; no implant has any role |\n\n**Where it sits in the escalation.** Book printed 81 and its tutorial box give the whole ladder: lubricants and ointment → **overnight taping** (the immediate measures of `opqb-t5-202`) → **gold weight implantation** or **tarsorrhaphy** → \"Lateral tarsorrhaphy & medial canthoplasty\" as the permanent measures → and always \"Treatment of the cause\".\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Gold weight = **upper lid that will not close**. If the question is a lid that will not open, the answer is a sling.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A gold weight implanted in the upper lid restores gravity-assisted closure in paralytic lagophthalmos; it is the opposite of what a ptotic lid needs.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.29 (Q206), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-207', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Xanthelasma** is most frequently located at:',
    options:['Lateral canthus','Medial eyelid region','Upper tarsus','Lower lid margin'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The book states the site twice, on consecutive pages.** Printed 79: \"It presents as a yellowish-white lesion found on the **medial aspect of the skin of the eyelids**.\" Printed 80: \"soft, yellow plaques filled with cholesterol at the **medial canthus** of the eyelids … multiple, **symmetric** lesions that more commonly are found on the **upper eyelid** than the lower one.\"\n\nSo the full description is **medial, bilateral, symmetric, upper more than lower, in the skin**. `L5) Eyelid disease` slide 34, headed \"Xanthelasma\" and carrying no other word, photographs precisely that: \"Flat-topped, soft, well-demarcated **YELLOW-CREAM PLAQUES** … at the **MEDIAL ends** of the lids on **BOTH SIDES** — most obvious on the upper lid of the RIGHT eye … with further yellow plaque in the right lower lid and matching, less raised, yellowish patches medially on the left upper and lower lids. The plaques lie **IN THE SKIN, well away from the lash line**, and the lid margins themselves are normal.\" The book's Fig (5-13) shows \"symmetric yellowish plaques at the medial aspects of **all four** lids\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and each is wrong for a different anatomical reason:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Lateral canthus** | The wrong end of the lid. Both sources say medial, and the difference is not incidental — it is what makes xanthelasma recognisable across a room |\n| **Upper tarsus** | The wrong **depth**. Xanthelasma is a **dermal** deposit — foam cells laden with cholesterol in the skin. The tarsus is the fibrous plate of the posterior lamella, and a lesion *in* it would be a chalazion or a meibomian gland tumour, not a plaque you can see on the surface |\n| **Lower lid margin** | Wrong on two counts: the **lower** lid is less often involved than the upper, and the lesion sits in the lid **skin away from the margin**, not on it. Lesions actually **on** the lid margin are a different differential entirely — stye, papilloma, and the malignancies |\n\n**Why the site is worth remembering as more than a fact.** \"Yellow plaque, medial, bilateral, skin\" is a complete diagnosis on inspection — the book says outright, printed 80, \"**Diagnosis is clinical.**\" Nothing else on the lid looks like it. And it converts the encounter into a systemic one: lipid profile, HbA1C, liver and thyroid function tests, because of the associations listed on printed 79–80 (hyperlipidaemia, diabetes mellitus, hypothyroidism, cardiovascular disease, obesity, heavy smoking) — and because the book's own systemic chapter, ch.19 printed 243, lists **xanthelasma among the \"Other effects of DM on the eye\"**.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** `opqb-t5-203` asks the treatment — surgical excision for cosmesis. This question asks the sign. Together they are the whole of what the module teaches about the lesion.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Xanthelasma sits in the skin at the medial aspect of the lids, bilaterally and symmetrically, upper more often than lower — a purely clinical diagnosis that should trigger lipid and metabolic testing.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.29 (Q207), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-208', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Trichiasis differs from entropion** because in trichiasis:',
    options:['Lashes are absent','Lid margin is normal','Lid margin is inverted','There is lagophthalmos'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The book states the discriminator in its opening line for trichiasis, and its cache flags that this is the only place in the chapter it is stated.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 78: \"The eyelid margin is **in its normal anatomic condition**, but the eyelashes are **misdirected** toward the eye, causnig [sic] irritation.\"\n\nSet against the entropion definition on printed 77 — \"The eyelid margin is **rotated inwards** toward the eye. This results in the lashes rubbing against the conjunctiva and cornea\" — the pair is exact:\n\n| | Trichiasis | Entropion |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Lid margin** | **Normal position** | **Rotated inward** |\n| **Lashes** | Individually misdirected | Normally sited, carried in with the margin |\n| **What must be treated** | The **lashes** — epilation, electrolysis, cryotherapy, grey-line graft (`opqb-t5-179`, `opqb-t5-221`, `opqb-t5-223`) | The **margin** — surgical rotation, everting sutures (`opqb-t5-176`, `opqb-t5-196`) |\n| **Symptoms** | Identical: red eye, foreign body sensation, reflex lacrimation | Identical |\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` photographs the contrast on consecutive slides with only the headings to separate them. Slide 27, \"Trichiasis\": an eye \"with a full, **normal, outward-growing set of UPPER lashes**, among which **ONE fine dark lash has turned BACKWARDS** and lies flat across the ocular surface\", and a second picture showing the consequence — misdirected lashes with \"BRIGHT GREEN FLUORESCEIN … staining the inferior cornea in a distinct green band\". Slide 29, \"Entropion / Senile\": \"The LOWER LID MARGIN IS ROLLED INWARD along its whole length: **the lash line has disappeared from view**\". The transcription notes that slide 27 prints nothing \"about which lid, what causes the misdirection, or the distinction from entropion — although slide 28 follows immediately\".\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Lashes are absent** | That is **madarosis**, keyed at `opqb-t5-186` and defined by the book as \"permanent loss of the lashes\" (printed 74). Trichiasis needs lashes to be present — misdirected ones |\n| **Lid margin is inverted** | The **entropion** definition, quoted verbatim above. Offering it as the trichiasis feature is a straight inversion of the answer |\n| **There is lagophthalmos** | A different malposition entirely — failure of closure (`opqb-t5-182`). Neither trichiasis nor entropion involves it |\n\n**Why the distinction is not academic.** The book notes on printed 78 that trichiasis is \"Associated with **entropion** or chronic inflammatory lid margin disease (**blepharitis**)\", so the two coexist — and treating the wrong one is the everyday error. Cryotherapy applied to lashes on a lid whose **margin** is inturned will destroy the lashes and leave the raw inturned margin still rubbing; rotating a margin that is already in the right place does nothing for a lash that is growing the wrong way. **Look at the margin first, then at the lashes.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'In trichiasis the lid margin is normally positioned and only the lashes are misdirected; in entropion the margin itself is rotated inward — which decides whether treatment targets the lashes or the margin.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.29 (Q208), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-209', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Posterior blepharitis** is mainly due to dysfunction of:',
    options:['Glands of Zeiss','Meibomian glands','Glands of Moll','Lacrimal gland'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Posterior blepharitis *is* meibomian gland dysfunction — the book prints the two as one heading.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 74: \"**2. Posterior Blepharitis (Meibomian Gland Dysfunction (MGD):** Alteration and **inspissation of meibomian gland secretions** contributing to ocular surface irritation, increased tear evaporation and an **unstable tear film**. It is a more chronic and persistent condition than anterior blepharitis.\"\n\n**Anterior versus posterior is a question of *which lamella*, and the meibomian glands sit in the tarsus — the posterior one.** Book printed 71: the tarsal plate is \"A rigid fibrous structure forming the skeleton of the eyelid and **containing the meibomian glands**\", which \"empty their oily secretions through orifices located **on the lid margin**\". Anterior blepharitis affects \"the area surrounding the bases of the eyelashes\" (printed 73) — the anterior lamella.\n\n**The clinical picture follows from the lipid.** Meibum is the oily outer layer of the tear film. When it thickens and the orifices plug, the tear film **evaporates** — hence the book's symptom list, printed 74: \"• **Dry eye manifestations** • **Recurrent chalazia**\", and its signs: \"**Hyperaemia and telangiectasia of the posterior lid margin** · **Foamy discharge** on the lid margin may be present · Either excessive abnormal (**turbid**) meibomian gland secretions or **inspissated secretions with plugged meibomian gland orifices**.\" Recurrent chalazia belong here for the obvious reason — a chalazion *is* an obstructed meibomian gland — and that is exactly what `opqb-t5-178` keys as the exception to the anterior list.\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` slide 26, headed \"Posterior Blepharitis / Meibomian Gland Dysfunction\", is the deck's most picture-dense slide: six photographs showing \"PALE YELLOW-WHITE **PLUGS** capping the meibomian orifices\", \"RAISED YELLOW-WHITE DOMES of inspissated secretion\", \"a band of **FROTHY WHITE FOAM** lying along the tear meniscus\", and an expression photograph in which \"thick, YELLOW, **TOOTHPASTE-LIKE** meibomian secretion is emerging in worm-like strands from several orifices at once\". No treatment and no lid-hygiene instruction is written on the slide.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Gland | Where | What it does, and what its disease is |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Zeis** | Sebaceous, attached to a **lash follicle**, anterior lamella | Its acute infection is the **stye** (`opqb-t5-212`); its chronic involvement is **anterior** blepharitis |\n| **Moll** | Modified apocrine **sweat** gland at the lash root, anterior lamella | Blockage gives a clear cyst; it contributes to the stye. Never posterior disease |\n| **Lacrimal** | In the superolateral **orbit**, not in the lid | Produces the aqueous layer of the tear film. Its disease is dacryoadenitis — an `op-lac` problem |\n\n⚠️ **Zeis and Moll are named nowhere in `L5`** — measured: zero hits in the deck's typed text and zero in every slide block, including the slide-2 anatomy diagram's label list, even though slides 17 and 20 are headed \"Stye (Hordeolum Externum)\" and \"Infected Chalazion / Hordeolum Internum\". The book carries them: Zeis in the hordeolum table on printed 72 and both as figure labels on printed 71.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Look at the **posterior** lid margin — plugged orifices, telangiectasia, foam on the tear meniscus — and treat with warm compresses, expression, lid hygiene and **oral tetracycline or doxycycline** (book printed 75), which alter meibum quality rather than merely killing bacteria.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Posterior blepharitis is meibomian gland dysfunction — inspissated tarsal-gland lipid causing evaporative dry eye and recurrent chalazia; Zeis and Moll are anterior-lamella lash glands.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.29 (Q209), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-210', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A **17-year-old student** presents with a **painful, red swelling at the margin of his upper eyelid for 2 days**. Examination shows a localized, tender swelling **at the lash line with a small yellow point and a lash at its apex**. Regarding management, which statement is **TRUE**?',
    options:['The lesion should be squeezed to drain pus','Oral antibiotics are mandatory in all cases','Topical antibiotic ointment and warm compresses are usually sufficient','Surgical incision through the conjunctiva is preferred'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**\"A lash at its apex\" is the book's own phrase, and it makes this a hordeolum externum — a stye.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 72: \"Painful, red, and tender swelling of the lid **pointing anteriorly through the skin on the lid margin usually with a lash at its apex**\", in \"Acute suppurative inflammation of the follicle of the lash or the associated gland of Zeiss … usually caused by *Staphylococcus aureus*\", and \"**common in children and young adults**\" — the stem's 17-year-old.\n\nThe book's treatment column answers all four options:\n\n> \"• **Topical antibiotic ointment on the lesion (Oral antibiotics are rarely needed).** • Pus may be evacuated by **epilation of the affected lash** or incision of the overlying skin. • **IT MUST BE NEVER SQUEEZED.** • Prevent recurrence by treating any associated local disease (e.g., blepharitis).\"\n\n**Why each option succeeds or fails:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Squeezed to drain pus** | ❌ The direct contradiction of the chapter's **only all-capital instruction**, printed in red: \"**IT MUST BE NEVER SQUEEZED**\". Squeezing forces infected material out of the walled-off follicle into the loose lid tissue, converting a stye into **preseptal cellulitis** — and, through the valveless facial and ophthalmic veins, carries the theoretical risk of **cavernous sinus thrombosis** *(that last mechanism is not taken from the course material — the book names cavernous sinus thrombosis only as a cause of lid swelling, printed 79)* |\n| **Oral antibiotics mandatory in all cases** | ❌ \"Oral antibiotics are **rarely needed**\" — the book's own parenthesis. Most styes point and discharge on warm compresses and a topical ointment. Systemic antibiotics belong to spreading infection: a lid cellulitis, or the whole-lid inflammation of `opqb-t5-218` |\n| **Topical antibiotic ointment and warm compresses** | ✅ The book's first line, and the mechanism is simple — heat softens the tissue and encourages the abscess to point and drain, ointment controls the staphylococcal load |\n| **Surgical incision through the conjunctiva** | ❌ Wrong lamella. This lesion is **anterior**: it is in a lash follicle, in front of the grey line, and it points **through the skin**. If it must be drained, the book allows **epilation of the affected lash** — the simplest drainage there is, pulling the lash out and opening the follicle — or \"incision of the **overlying skin**\". The **conjunctival** route belongs to the lesions of the posterior lamella: chalazion (`opqb-t5-193`) and hordeolum internum (`opqb-t5-188`) |\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` slide 17, headed \"Stye (Hordeolum Externum)\", photographs it: \"a localised, red, tender swelling at the OUTER part of the UPPER lid margin, **centred on the lash line**, with a **yellow pointing head** at the lid margin … The lesion sits at the base of a lash — the anterior lamella origin is what the picture shows.\" ⚠️ The deck \"prints NO organism, NO treatment and NO distinction in words between external and internal hordeolum\".\n\n**Note on the printing.** The source runs the stem's last sentence on in lower case — \"lash at its apex. regarding management, which statement is TRUE?\"\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Warm compresses and ointment; **never squeeze**; epilate the lash if it needs draining; and **look at the lid margin afterwards**, because recurrent styes mean underlying blepharitis — the rule keyed at `opqb-t5-219`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A stye is treated with warm compresses and topical antibiotic ointment, drained if necessary by epilating the affected lash — never squeezed, rarely needing oral antibiotics, and never incised through the conjunctiva.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.29 (Q210), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-211', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'Which of the following features **differentiates chalazion from hordeolum**?',
    options:['Associated pain','Pointing to lid margin','Acute onset','Painless, chronic course'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The two lesions differ on the same two axes — acute versus chronic, and infected versus sterile — and the first three options all describe the hordeolum.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` printed 73, chalazion: \"**Sterile chronic** granulomatous inflammatory lesion of the meibomian glands caused by retained sebaceous secretions\", presenting as a \"Gradually enlarging **painless** rounded nodule\" that is \"A firm nodule **within the tarsal plate**\". Printed 72, both hordeola: \"**Acute** suppurative inflammation …\", \"**Painful**, red, and tender swelling of the lid\", \"Associated with dull, throbbing pain\".\n\nThe book's second flowchart, on printed 72, draws the axis that the prose never states — and the cache flags it as figure-only teaching:\n\n> Inflammation → **Localized** → **Acute** — Hordeolum externum / Hordeolum internum\n> Inflammation → **Localized** → **Chronic** — Chalazion Meibomian\n\n| Feature | Chalazion | Hordeolum |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Pain** | **Painless** | **Painful, tender, throbbing** |\n| **Onset / course** | **Gradual, chronic** — weeks to months | **Acute** — days |\n| **Nature** | **Sterile** lipogranuloma | **Suppurative** staphylococcal infection |\n| **Position** | **Within the tarsal plate**, away from the margin | **At the margin** — externum at the lash line, internum deep to it |\n| **Points** | On the **conjunctival** side, behind the grey line | Externum **through the skin**, \"usually with a lash at its apex\" |\n| **Treatment** | Warm compresses; intralesional steroid; incision and curettage from the conjunctival side | Warm compresses; topical antibiotic; epilation of the lash |\n\n**Why the first three options are hordeolum features:**\n\n| Option | Whose feature it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Associated pain** | The hordeolum's — \"dull, throbbing pain\", printed 72. A **painful** chalazion has become **infected** and is now behaving as a hordeolum internum, which is the situation of `opqb-t5-215` |\n| **Pointing to lid margin** | The hordeolum externum's, at the lash line. The chalazion sits **within the tarsus, away from the margin** — the cache notes that its position away from the margin is shown in all three of the book's photographs on printed 73 and stated in prose only as \"within the tarsal plate\" |\n| **Acute onset** | The hordeolum's. Days, not months |\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` sets the two side by side in pictures. Slide 18, \"Chalazion\": \"a smooth, rounded, **painless-looking** swelling in the middle of the lid, skin over it normal-coloured and **not inflamed**\", with two further panels showing the lesion pointing on the everted conjunctival surface. Slide 17, \"Stye\", and slide 20, \"Infected Chalazion / Hordeolum Internum\": red, tense, yellow-pointing, with surrounding oedema.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** \"Painless and slow\" versus \"painful and fast\" settles it at the bedside, and the two attributes travel together because they have the same cause: no organism, no pain, no hurry. `opqb-t5-177` keys the chalazion as the commonest **painless** lid swelling on exactly this basis.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A chalazion is a painless, chronic, sterile tarsal lipogranuloma; a hordeolum is an acute, painful, suppurative infection pointing at the lid margin.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.30 (Q211), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-212', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Hordeolum externum** is an infection of:',
    options:['Meibomian gland','Glands of Zeiss or Moll','Lacrimal gland','Conjunctival gland'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The book's definition names the lash follicle and Zeis; the bank adds Moll, which shares the same site.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 72: \"**Hordeolum Externum (stye) — Acute suppurative inflammation of the follicle of the lash or the associated gland of Zeiss.** It is usually caused by *Staphylococcus aureus*.\"\n\n**The anterior lamella's three structures, all at the lash root:**\n\n| Structure | Type | Book |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Lash follicle** | Hair follicle | printed 72, named in the definition |\n| **Gland of Zeis** | **Sebaceous**, opening into the follicle | printed 72 (\"Zeiss\") and the printed-71 figure (\"Gland of Zeis\") |\n| **Gland of Moll** | Modified **apocrine sweat** gland | printed 71, a **figure label only** — the cache records that Moll is \"never mentioned in prose anywhere in the chapter\" |\n\nAll three sit **in front of the tarsus**, in the anterior leaflet — which is why a stye points **anteriorly through the skin on the lid margin, usually with a lash at its apex** (printed 72, and the vignette at `opqb-t5-210`), and why it can be drained simply by **epilating the affected lash**: pulling the lash out opens the very follicle the pus is in.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Where it is | What its infection is called |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Meibomian gland** | **In the tarsus** — posterior lamella | Acute infection = **hordeolum internum** (`opqb-t5-188`); chronic sterile obstruction = **chalazion** (`opqb-t5-167`); chronic dysfunction = **posterior blepharitis** (`opqb-t5-209`). Three separate diseases, all of the same gland, none of them the stye |\n| **Lacrimal gland** | Superolateral **orbit** | **Dacryoadenitis** — the S-shaped ptosis and superolateral pain of `opqb-t4-155` in the shipped corpus. Not a lid lesion |\n| **Conjunctival gland** | The accessory lacrimal glands (Krause, Wolfring) lie in the fornix and at the upper tarsal border | They secrete tears. They do not produce hordeola. ⚠️ Wolfring is a **figure label** on book printed 71 and Krause is never printed at all |\n\n⚠️ **Neither eponym appears in the lecture deck.** \"Zeis\" and \"Moll\" return **zero** across all 170 typed words of `L5) Eyelid disease` and zero in every one of its slide blocks — even though slide 17 is headed \"Stye (Hordeolum Externum)\" and slide 20 \"Infected Chalazion / Hordeolum Internum\". The deck teaches the two hordeola entirely by photograph and heading; the glands come from the book.\n\n**Note on the printing.** The source sets \"Glands of Zeiss\" (the book does the same on printed 72, while its own figure on printed 71 spells it \"Gland of Zeis\") — both spellings are the sources' own.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Front of the grey line, at the lash root → **stye**, drain by epilation or through skin. Behind it, in the tarsus → **internal hordeolum or chalazion**, drain through conjunctiva. The corpus asks where the incision goes at `opmcq-c1-1`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Hordeolum externum is acute staphylococcal infection of a lash follicle and its glands of Zeis and Moll in the anterior lamella; the meibomian gland lies in the tarsus and gives the internal hordeolum and chalazion.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.30 (Q212), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-213', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Intralesional steroid injection** is indicated in:',
    options:['Hordeolum externum','Hordeolum internum','Chalazion','Blepharitis'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The book lists it as the first of the chalazion's surgical treatments.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 73: \"2. **Surgical treatment:** • **Intralesional steroid injection** • Incision and curettage through the conjunctival side • A recurrent chalazion (especially in elderly individuals) should be biopsied to exclude an underlying malignancy (meibomian gland carcinoma).\"\n\n**Why steroid works on a chalazion and on nothing else in this list.** The chalazion is a **sterile granuloma** — the book's own word, printed 73: \"**Sterile** chronic **granulomatous** inflammatory lesion of the meibomian glands caused by retained sebaceous secretions.\" A granuloma is a mass of macrophages and giant cells walling off leaked lipid, and corticosteroid **suppresses exactly that cellular response**, so the lesion involutes without an incision, without a clamp and without a scar. It is particularly useful for lesions near the **punctum or the lid margin**, where cutting risks damaging the drainage apparatus or notching the margin.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong, and two of them for the same important reason:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Hordeolum externum** | An **acute suppurative infection** of a lash follicle (book printed 72). Injecting steroid into an untreated bacterial abscess suppresses the local immune response and lets it spread. Its treatment is warm compresses, topical antibiotic ointment and, if it needs draining, **epilation of the affected lash** (`opqb-t5-210`) |\n| **Hordeolum internum** | Also an **acute suppurative infection**, this time of a meibomian gland. Same objection. The book adds that it \"should **NEVER** be incised during active inflammation **UNLESS** it forms an abscess\" — active infection is a reason to be *less* invasive, not more (`opqb-t5-188`) |\n| **Blepharitis** | A **chronic diffuse** disease of the lid margin, not a discrete lesion — there is nothing to inject *into*. Its treatment is lid hygiene with topical or oral antibiotics (`opqb-t5-205`). The book allows **topical** steroid drops only in posterior blepharitis, \"to control ocular surface inflammation\" (printed 75), which is a different route and a different indication |\n\n**The rule underneath all four.** Steroid into a **sterile inflammatory** mass is treatment; steroid into an **infected** one is a mistake. That single principle sorts the whole list.\n\n⚠️ **And the limit on it, which the next question depends on.** Steroid is for a *first or second* chalazion. A chalazion that keeps coming back in the same place is the one the book says must be **biopsied**, because meibomian gland carcinoma presents as a recurrent chalazion — the rule `opqb-t5-222` keys and `opqb-t5-193` uses as a distractor. Repeating an injection into a recurrent lesion is how a carcinoma gets treated as a cyst.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Intralesional steroid is a treatment for the chalazion — a sterile granuloma — and is contraindicated in the acutely infected hordeola; a recurrent chalazion needs biopsy, not another injection.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.30 (Q213), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-214', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'In the previous question, the condition for which **intralesional steroid injection** is indicated was the **chalazion**. Its common association is:',
    options:['Posterior blepharitis','Xanthelasma','Lagophthalmos','Epicanthus'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ BACK-REFERENCE, and a two-step one. The bank's printed stem is \"In the previous Q, common association of Previous condition is:\". \"The previous Q\" is `opqb-t5-213`, and \"the previous condition\" is not that question's stem but its **answer** — the chalazion. Both links were read on the page. The stem above restates the antecedent so this entry stands alone.)*\n\n**The book prints the association in the chalazion's own Incidence paragraph.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 73: \"• The most common benign non-neoplastic lesion of the eyelids • **Commonly associated with posterior or seborrheic blepharitis.**\"\n\n**Why posterior blepharitis and the chalazion are the same disease at two scales.** A chalazion is an **obstructed meibomian gland**. Posterior blepharitis *is* meibomian gland dysfunction — the book's definition, printed 74: \"Alteration and **inspissation of meibomian gland secretions**\", with signs of \"**inspissated secretions with plugged meibomian gland orifices**\". Thicken the meibum and plug the orifices across the whole lid, and sooner or later one gland dams up completely, its retained lipid leaks into the tarsus, and a lipogranuloma forms. So MGD is the field and the chalazion is the event.\n\nThe book closes the loop from the other direction: **recurrent chalazia** is one of only two symptoms it gives for posterior blepharitis (printed 74, beside \"Dry eye manifestations\") — which is precisely why `opqb-t5-178` keys \"recurrent chalazion\" as the item that does **not** belong to the anterior list.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it is associated with |\n|---|---|\n| **Xanthelasma** | A dermal **cholesterol** deposit at the medial lids, associated with **lipid disorders, diabetes, hypothyroidism, cardiovascular disease, obesity and smoking** (book printed 79–80). Its lipid is in the *skin*, the chalazion's in the *tarsus* — the two are unrelated |\n| **Lagophthalmos** | Associated with **facial nerve palsy**, cicatricial lid shortening, sedation in ICU and proptosis (book printed 80–81). A closure problem, not a glandular one |\n| **Epicanthus** | A congenital **skin fold** at the medial canthus, associated with a broad flat nasal bridge in childhood (book printed 79). Nothing to do with glands |\n\n**Clinical bottom line, and it is the reason the association is worth testing.** Treating a chalazion without treating the lid margin behind it guarantees the next one. The book's own instruction for the neighbouring lesion says the same thing in one line, printed 72: \"**Prevent recurrence by treating any associated local disease (e.g., blepharitis).**\" That is exactly the answer keyed at `opqb-t5-219`, and the management is warm compresses, lid hygiene and oral tetracycline or doxycycline (printed 75).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Chalazion is commonly associated with posterior or seborrhoeic blepharitis, because meibomian gland dysfunction is the field in which an individual gland obstructs.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.30 (Q214), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-215', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A patient presenting with a **swelling in his upper lid for one year that does not change in size** and is **now becoming red and painful**. The **first line of management** is:',
    options:['Systemic and local antibiotics.','Immediate histo-pathological study.','Performing a CT scan.','Simple excision'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**A year-old, unchanging, painless lid swelling is a chalazion; a chalazion that turns red and painful has become secondarily infected — and infection is treated before anything is cut.** `L5) Eyelid disease` names the entity in a heading: slide 20 is titled \"**Infected Chalazion / Hordeolum Internum**\", photographing \"a large, tense, red-and-yellow swelling within the substance of the lid, well BEHIND the lash line, with a pale pointing centre\".\n\n**Why antibiotics come first, and why cutting now would be wrong.** The book's rule for the acutely inflamed meibomian lesion is unambiguous, printed 72: \"It should **NEVER** be incised during active inflammation **UNLESS** it forms an abscess\" — both words underlined in the original. Cutting into an actively inflamed lid spreads infection through the loose tissue planes, bleeds into an oedematous field, and gives a poor scar. Settle the infection first; then, once the lid is quiet, deal with whatever is left.\n\nThe book's own treatment column for hordeolum internum supplies the regimen: \"Warm compresses with gentle massage · Topical antibiotic eye drops, or ointments … · **Oral antigram positive antibiotics and analgesics**\" (printed 72) — i.e. **systemic and local antibiotics**, which is what option A says.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong *at this moment*:**\n\n| Option | Why not now |\n|---|---|\n| **Immediate histopathological study** | ⚠️ Not wrong in principle — wrong in **timing**. The book does want tissue from a **recurrent** chalazion: \"A recurrent chalazion (especially in elderly individuals) should be **biopsied** to exclude an underlying malignancy (meibomian gland carcinoma)\" (printed 73). But this lesion has been *stable for a year and has just become infected*, which is the behaviour of a chalazion, not of a growing tumour. Biopsy belongs after the inflammation has settled — and it is exactly what `opqb-t5-222` keys for the lesion that has recurred three times |\n| **CT scan** | Imaging is for **orbital** disease — a mass behind the septum, proptosis, restricted motility, an orbital fracture. A palpable lesion in the substance of the lid is diagnosed by looking at it and feeling it |\n| **Simple excision** | The right operation on the wrong day, and it also misses the point: a chalazion is not \"simply excised\" — the book's operation is \"**Incision and curettage through the conjunctival side**\" (printed 73), the route keyed at `opqb-t5-193` |\n\n⚠️ **The reassurance that must not be over-read.** \"Does not change in size\" over a year is against malignancy but is not proof of anything. The features that would override the sequence above are **lash loss over the lesion, ulceration, a pearly rolled edge, distortion of the lid margin, or fixity to skin** — and the book's own audit records that its chapter names **no** eyelid malignancy and gives no warning-sign list at all, its malignancies living in ch.17 (printed 214–217). `L5` photographs several of those signs across its ten silent tumour slides and names none of them. *(The warning-sign list is not taken from the course material.)*\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** **Infected now → antibiotics now.** Definitive treatment, and the decision whether to send the tissue, comes when the lid is quiet.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A long-standing chalazion that becomes red and painful is secondarily infected and is treated first with systemic and topical antibiotics; it is never incised during active inflammation unless an abscess forms.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.30 (Q215), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-216', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A patient with a history of a **skin cut wound in the left cheek** presents with **inability to close this eye properly**. The most serious complication to guard against is:',
    options:['Corneal scarring.','Exposure corneal ulcer.','Corneal pannus.','Corneal vascularization.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A cut in the cheek that leaves the eye unable to close has divided branches of the facial nerve, and the danger is what happens to the uncovered cornea.** The facial nerve's temporal and zygomatic branches run superficially across the face to reach orbicularis oculi; a laceration on their course paralyses lid closure — paralytic lagophthalmos. The book's aetiology 1, printed 80, names \"**Trauma** (fractures to the skull base or mandible, surgery as in post-ptosis or blepharoplasty surgeries)\" among the causes of facial nerve damage, and states the consequence in its definition: \"The **ocular surface become subject to damage from exposure**\" (printed 80).\n\n**Why an ulcer rather than a scar is the answer.** The option list is a sequence, and the question asks what to **guard against** — the event that has to be prevented:\n\n| Option | Where it sits in the sequence |\n|---|---|\n| **Exposure corneal ulcer** | ✅ The **acute, preventable, sight-threatening** event. The lower third of the cornea dries, the epithelium breaks down, and a sterile exposure ulcer becomes a **bacterial keratitis** with the risk of perforation and endophthalmitis. This is the emergency |\n| **Corneal scarring** | The **end result** of that ulcer once it has happened — a permanent opacity. You do not \"guard against\" the scar; you guard against the ulcer that makes it. The book's neighbouring entry uses exactly this logic for entropion: \"corneal abrasions with secondary corneal scarring. (**If infectious keratitis occurs, it may lead to loss of vision**)\" (printed 77) |\n| **Corneal pannus** | A fibrovascular membrane growing down from the limbus — the hallmark of **trachoma** and of chronic surface disease and contact-lens hypoxia. A late, chronic response, not the acute danger here |\n| **Corneal vascularization** | Likewise a **chronic** response to prolonged hypoxia or inflammation. It threatens clarity over months, not the eye over days |\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` slide 15, \"Lagophthalmos / Paralytic\", photographs the mechanism: a woman attempting to close both eyes, one side paralysed, \"the lids remain apart with the globe rolled up so only white sclera and the lower cornea show\", the nasolabial fold flattened and the mouth pulled to the other side. ⚠️ The deck prints no exposure-keratopathy warning anywhere — \"Bell\" returns zero across all 170 of its typed words.\n\n**What protects the eye in the meantime** — book printed 81, and the answer keyed at `opqb-t5-202`: prophylactic **lubrication with artificial tears**, **ointment and taping of the lids at bedtime**, \"Treatment of associated corneal exposure or ulceration\", and if closure does not return, **tarsorrhaphy** or **gold weight implantation** (`opqb-t5-206`). And **Bell's phenomenon** — the globe rolling up and in on attempted closure (printed 80) — is what buys some of these patients time; a patient without it is at much greater risk.\n\n⚠️ **Note on the printing.** The source reads \"a skin cut wound in the **It.** cheek\" — a capital L read as a capital I; *Lt.* = left. Repaired above.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Facial nerve injury from a facial laceration is an **ophthalmic** emergency as much as a cosmetic one. Examine the lower cornea and start lubricating before the surgeon has finished suturing the cheek.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A facial laceration paralysing lid closure threatens an exposure corneal ulcer, the acute preventable event; scarring, pannus and vascularisation are its later consequences.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.30 (Q216), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-217', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'The **most important examination** in cases presenting with **congenital ptosis** is:',
    options:['The function of the levator muscle','The state of the extraocular muscles.','The examination of the cranial nerves','The examination of the fundus.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Levator function is the measurement that selects the operation, and the lecture puts it on the only two slides in the whole deck that carry body text.** `L5) Eyelid disease` slide 8: \"**Levator resection — Indicated for any ptosis provided levator function is at least 5 mm.**\" Slide 9: \"**Frontalis brow suspension — Main indications: · Severe ptosis with poor levator function ( 4 mm or less ) · Marcus Gunn jaw-winking syndrome.**\" The deck's own audit records that \"levator\" occurs five times in its 170 typed words, all of them on slides 8–9, and \"always as the criterion for an operation\".\n\nThe book prints the same rule inside the surgical plate on printed 76 — \"**Levator resection / Good levator function**\" against \"**Frontalis sling / Poor levator function**\" — and its own aetiology list is built round the same axis: congenital ptosis is \"Due to lack of development of levator muscle, resulting in a smooth lid with loss of tarsal fold **[poor levator function]**\", while aponeurotic ptosis \"Typically shows a **good levator function**\" (printed 76).\n\n**How it is measured**, from `L5` slide 7, which photographs the manoeuvre without a word: the examiner's **thumb pressed firmly on the brow** to block frontalis — otherwise the brow lifts the lid and the reading is falsely high — a millimetre ruler held against the upper lid, zeroed on the lid margin in **full downgaze**, and read again in **full upgaze**. Normal is about 15–18 mm (`opqb-t5-190`); the thresholds are 5 mm and 4 mm.\n\n**Why the other three are not the *most* important — and none of them is worthless:**\n\n| Option | Its real value |\n|---|---|\n| **The state of the extraocular muscles** | ⚠️ Genuinely important, and second on the list. The levator and superior rectus share an embryological origin, so a dysplastic levator often comes with a **weak superior rectus** on the same side — which is why `opqb-t5-187` keys extraocular involvement as *not* rare. It also matters for **Bell's phenomenon**: a child with poor elevation is at greater risk of exposure after a big lift. But it modifies the plan; it does not choose the operation |\n| **The examination of the cranial nerves** | The way to exclude a **third-nerve palsy** or a **Horner's syndrome** — a ptosis with an abnormal pupil is not congenital dystrophy and does not go to the operating theatre first. Essential to *diagnosis*, but the stem already says the ptosis is congenital |\n| **The examination of the fundus** | Sensible in any child, and mandatory if there is reduced vision — but nothing in the fundus tells you how to lift a lid |\n\n**The examination the option list omits, and it is the one that decides *whether* to operate at all:** whether the lid is **covering the visual axis**, because that is the amblyopia risk, and it is what `opqb-t5-187` keys as the surgical indication. ⚠️ Amblyopia is named nowhere in `L5` — measured across all 170 typed words — and nowhere in the book's ptosis section.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** **Is the pupil covered?** decides whether to operate and when. **What is the levator function?** decides which operation. Everything else refines the answer.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Levator function is the key measurement in congenital ptosis because it selects the operation — ≥5 mm levator resection, ≤4 mm frontalis sling.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.30 (Q217), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-218', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A **13-year-old female** patient complains of **recurrent multiple painful swellings on the left eye lids**. On examination, visual acuity was 6/12 and 6/18. The **whole lower lid was red, swollen and tender**. The treatment of this case is:',
    options:['Systemic non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs.','Topical antibiotic drops.','Surgical incision.','Systemic and topical antibiotics and hot fomentation.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Painful, multiple and recurrent means staphylococcal infection; a *whole lid* that is red, swollen and tender means the infection is no longer confined to one gland.** Individual hordeola are discrete — the book's externum is a \"localized\" swelling \"pointing anteriorly through the skin on the lid margin\" (printed 72). When the entire lower lid is involved, the process has spread into the lid tissue as a **cellulitis of the lid**, and the book lists exactly that among its causes of lid swelling, printed 79: \"4. **Inflammation:** – **Cellulitis of the skin of the lid** or other lid inflammations (**stye/Hordeolum internum**)\".\n\n**Why the answer is all three components together:**\n\n| Component | Why it is needed |\n|---|---|\n| **Systemic antibiotics** | Diffuse lid involvement will not be reached by drops. The book prescribes \"**Oral antigram positive antibiotics and analgesics**\" for hordeolum internum (printed 72) and \"Systemic antibiotics (tetracyclines doxycycline, azithromycin)\" for staphylococcal blepharitis (printed 74) |\n| **Topical antibiotics** | Treats the surface and the colonised **lid margin** that is generating the recurrences |\n| **Hot fomentation** | The book's own first-line physical measure for both hordeola — \"Warm compresses with gentle massage\" (printed 72). Heat brings a pointing lesion to the surface and softens inspissated meibum |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Systemic NSAIDs** | Treat the pain and none of the cause. This is a bacterial infection |\n| **Topical antibiotic drops alone** | Under-treatment: drops do not penetrate a diffusely inflamed lid, and the systemic component is what this presentation needs |\n| **Surgical incision** | ⚠️ **The trap, and the book forbids it in terms:** the internal hordeolum \"should **NEVER** be incised during **active inflammation** **UNLESS** it forms an abscess\" (printed 72, both words underlined). There is no localised abscess described here — the whole lid is involved. Cutting spreads it |\n\n⚠️ **A detail the stem gives and does not explain.** The two acuities, 6/12 and 6/18, are printed without saying which eye is which, and **nothing in an infected lower lid accounts for them** — lid cellulitis does not reduce vision, and reduced vision in a lid infection would instead raise the question of **orbital** rather than preseptal involvement. Recorded as printed, without inventing an explanation. (⚠️ Preseptal versus orbital cellulitis is **absent** from the book's eyelid chapter, which mentions \"cellulitis of the skin of the lid\" once, in the cause table on printed 79; the book names preseptal cellulitis in ch.4, printed 67. `L5` returns zero for \"cellulit\", \"preseptal\" and \"orbital\" across all 170 of its typed words, although it has a slide headed \"Lid Swelling (Edema))\".)\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Localised and pointing → topical treatment and, if it must be drained, epilate the lash or wait for the abscess (`opqb-t5-210`, `opqb-t5-215`). **Diffuse lid** → systemic antibiotics. And ask, every time, **why it keeps happening** — which is what Q219 asks next.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Recurrent multiple lid abscesses with a diffusely red, swollen, tender lid need systemic plus topical antibiotics and hot fomentation; incision during active inflammation is contraindicated without an abscess.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.30 (Q218), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-219', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'In the above mentioned case — the **13-year-old with recurrent multiple painful lid swellings** and a diffusely red, swollen, tender lower lid — **recurrence may be prevented by**:',
    options:['A maintenance dose of systemic antibiotics','A maintenance dose of vitamins.','Treatment of blepharitis.','Long term use of topical antibiotics'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ BACK-REFERENCE, phrased \"In the above mentioned case\" rather than \"the previous Q\". The antecedent is `opqb-t5-218`, one question back, read and confirmed on the page; it is restated above so this entry stands alone.)*\n\n**The book gives the rule in one line, in the stye's treatment column.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 72: \"**Prevent recurrence by treating any associated local disease (e.g., blepharitis).**\"\n\n**Why blepharitis is the reservoir.** Recurrent styes are a **complication of chronic staphylococcal blepharitis** — the book lists \"**Recurrent styes**\" among its complications on printed 74, and `opqb-t5-204` keys that chain. The organism lives in the crusts and collarettes at the lash bases; from there it repeatedly seeds the follicles and their glands of Zeis. Sterilise a single stye and the colonised margin is still there. Clean and treat the **margin**, and the supply is cut off. The same logic runs on the posterior side: MGD is why chalazia recur (`opqb-t5-214`).\n\n**What \"treatment of blepharitis\" actually means**, from the book's three treatment lists (printed 73, 74, 75): **lid hygiene — warm compresses and lid scrubs with diluted baby shampoo** as the constant; **topical fusidic acid or azithromycin** for the staphylococcal form; **oral tetracyclines, doxycycline or azithromycin** where topical measures fail or the disease is posterior; treatment of associated **scalp seborrhoea**; and management of complications. Lid hygiene is the part that continues indefinitely.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Maintenance systemic antibiotics** | Treats the patient rather than the reservoir, and does so at the price of resistance and adverse effects. Note the distinction from the book's own use of **oral doxycycline in posterior blepharitis**, which is prescribed for its effect on **meibum quality and lid-margin inflammation**, not as indefinite antibacterial prophylaxis |\n| **Maintenance vitamins** | No basis in either source. Vitamin A deficiency is a real ocular surface disease, but it causes xerophthalmia and keratomalacia, not recurrent styes |\n| **Long term use of topical antibiotics** | Closer, but still the wrong target and the wrong duration: it suppresses organisms without removing the **crusts, collarettes and inspissated meibum** they live in, and long-term use selects resistance. Lid hygiene is the mechanical part that antibiotics cannot replace |\n\n**Clinical bottom line — the rule for the whole inflammatory half of this topic.** A single stye or chalazion is an event; **recurrence is a diagnosis**, and the diagnosis is at the lid margin. Two exceptions to remember: recurrence in an **elderly** patient in the **same site** is the book's biopsy indication for meibomian gland carcinoma (printed 73, keyed at `opqb-t5-222`), and recurrent chalazia should also prompt a look for the posterior blepharitis behind them.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Recurrent styes and chalazia are prevented by treating the underlying blepharitis — lid hygiene with topical or oral antibiotics — not by maintenance antibiotics or vitamins.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.30 (Q219), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-220', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A **16-year-old female** patient was complaining of **presence of scales on her lashes**. On examination the **skin of the face was greasy** and she had **dandruff on the eyebrows**. This patient is suffering from:',
    options:['Meibomianitis.','Angular blepharitis.','Contact dermatitis.','Seborrheic blepharitis'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The lid disease is diagnosed from the skin outside it.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 73, anterior blepharitis type a: \"**Seborrheic:** – **Associated with generalised seborrheic dermatitis** – Characterized by: • Hyperaemic lid margin • The presence of **greasy soft scales on the lashes. (no ulceration)** – Treatment: 1. Lid hygiene (warm compresses and lid scrubs with diluted baby shampoo) 2. **treatment of associated scalp seborrhoea**.\"\n\nEvery element of the stem maps onto that entry: **scales on the lashes** (the lid sign), **greasy facial skin** and **dandruff on the eyebrows** (the generalised seborrhoeic dermatitis it is associated with). The lid is one site of a skin condition, which is why the book's treatment has two halves and the second half is dermatological.\n\n`L5) Eyelid disease` slide 23, headed \"Anterior Blepharitis / Seborrheic\" and printing nothing else, photographs the lid sign four times: \"Soft, greasy **WHITE SCALES** are wrapped round the lash **SHAFTS** a short way up from their bases, **sleeving** them; the margin skin between the lashes is intact, **NOT ulcerated**, and the lashes themselves are long and unbroken.\"\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and the whole question is a four-way discrimination of lid-margin disease:**\n\n| Option | Where the disease is | The finding that excludes it here |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Meibomianitis** | The **posterior** margin — meibomian gland dysfunction | Gives plugged and capped orifices, telangiectasia of the posterior margin, **foamy** discharge on the tear meniscus and recurrent chalazia (book printed 74). Not scales sleeving the lash shafts, and it has no association with facial seborrhoea |\n| **Angular blepharitis** | The **canthi** only | *Moraxella* macerates the skin at the inner and outer angles and spares the lash line (`opqb-t5-166`, `opqb-t5-175`). The stem's disease is on the lashes and generalised over the face |\n| **Contact dermatitis** | The lid **skin** | An eczematous reaction — itchy, oedematous, weeping or lichenified skin, often with a history of a new cosmetic or drop. It does not produce greasy scales along the lash line, and it does not give eyebrow dandruff |\n\n⚠️ **The one discrimination this question is *not* making, and it is the one to be sure of:** seborrhoeic versus **staphylococcal (ulcerative)** anterior blepharitis. Both put material on the lash bases. The book separates them by texture and by damage — \"**greasy soft scales** … **(no ulceration)**\" for the seborrhoeic type (printed 73), against \"**Hard dry scales** around the bases of lashes (**collarettes**)\" with \"**ulceration of the lid margin**\" and \"Lashes fall with scarring and notching\" for the staphylococcal (printed 74). The cache records that this contrast is visible only by comparing the two pages' photographs — **neither page states it in words**. `L5` slides 22 and 23 repeat the same silent comparison.\n\n**Note on the printing.** The source sets \"Seborrheic blepharitis\" without a full stop, using the same spelling as the book.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Greasy soft scales, intact lashes, no ulcer, and a greasy scalp and brows → **seborrhoeic**: lid hygiene plus treat the scalp. Hard collarettes, an ulcerated notched margin and lashes falling → **staphylococcal**: lid hygiene plus antibiotics, and expect the complications of `opqb-t5-204`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Greasy soft scales sleeving the lashes with a non-ulcerated margin, in a patient with facial and eyebrow seborrhoea, is seborrhoeic anterior blepharitis — treated by lid hygiene plus treatment of the scalp.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.31 (Q220), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-221', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'**Epilation** of maldirected rubbing eyelashes is indicated:',
    options:['When the number of mal-directed eyelashes is less than six.','When the mal-directed eyelashes are present close to each other.','When there is an acute corneal condition.','When the number is more than six.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Epilation is the one trichiasis treatment that works immediately and lasts only weeks — so its indication is the situation that needs relief now.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 78 lists it first and marks it: \"1. **Repeated epilation of the offending lashes. (Temporary → Tutorial )**\". The word the book chooses is **temporary**, and the cache warns that the tutorial marker \"covers only the temporariness\" while the item itself is core text. A lash pulled out of an intact follicle **grows back in about four to six weeks**, and grows back in the same wrong direction — which is why the book says *repeated* epilation.\n\n**Why that makes an acute corneal problem the indication.** If the cornea is abraded, ulcerating or staining — the picture `L5) Eyelid disease` slide 27 photographs, with misdirected lashes and \"**BRIGHT GREEN FLUORESCEIN** … staining the inferior cornea in a distinct green band\" — the lashes have to come off the eye **today**. Epilation needs nothing but forceps, takes seconds, and buys the weeks in which the epithelium heals and a definitive treatment can be arranged. Electrolysis, cryotherapy and surgery are all definitive and all take longer to arrange and to work.\n\n⚠️ **This key is unusual and is recorded as printed, not disputed.** The customary teaching — and the reading option A offers — is that epilation suits a **few** scattered lashes, with cryotherapy for many; the bank itself keys cryotherapy for **ten** lashes at `opqb-t5-223`, which is the same rule from the other end. So a candidate choosing A has understood the standard teaching. The bank keys **C**, and C stands as the `answer` here; the book supports its logic — epilation as a **temporary, holding** measure — without ever stating a number.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong on the bank's reading:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Less than six lashes** | ⚠️ The standard indication, and the strongest distractor. But a *number* describes how much work the treatment is, not the *reason* to choose a temporary one over a permanent one. ⚠️ **No number appears in either source** — the book prints no count anywhere in its trichiasis entry, and `L5` slide 27 prints nothing at all about treatment |\n| **Lashes present close to each other** | Clustered lashes are an argument for treating a **segment** at once — cryotherapy or surgery — rather than plucking them one by one |\n| **More than six lashes** | The direct opposite of the customary teaching: many lashes make epilation impractical and short-lived, and are the classic indication for **cryotherapy** — which is exactly what `opqb-t5-223` keys |\n\n**The full ladder, from the book's printed list on printed 78** and the bank's own box at `opqb-t5-179`: **1. repeated epilation · 2. electrolysis of the lash follicles · 3. cryotherapy · 4. surgery** — the last being a mucous-membrane graft into a groove cut along the **grey line**, a description that exists only inside the book's tutorial marker and in two hand-drawn figure panels.\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Epilation relieves; it does not cure. Any patient epilated should leave with a plan for **electrolysis, cryotherapy or surgery**, or they will be back in six weeks.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Epilation is the immediate, temporary trichiasis treatment — lashes regrow in weeks — so it is used to relieve an acutely damaged cornea while definitive follicle destruction is arranged.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.31 (Q221), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-222', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A **52-year-old** patient with **recurrent chalazion (three times)** in the **upper** eye lid should be managed by:',
    options:['Antibiotics and steroids.','Systemic antibiotics and anti-histamines.','Excision and curette operation.','Excision and histopathology.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**The book prints this rule as its only oncological sentence in twelve pages, and the word that triggers it is *recurrent*.** `ophthalmology.pdf` printed 73: \"A **recurrent chalazion** (especially in elderly individuals) should be **biopsied to exclude an underlying malignancy (meibomian gland carcinoma)**.\"\n\n**Why a recurring chalazion is a red flag.** A chalazion is a blocked gland: drain it and it should not come back **in the same place**. A lesion that reappears three times in the same site is behaving like something that was never fully removed — and **sebaceous (meibomian) gland carcinoma is the great masquerader of the eyelid**, presenting for months as a firm painless tarsal nodule indistinguishable from a chalazion, and being curetted repeatedly while it grows. It arises from the meibomian glands, so it is a **tarsal** lesion; it has a predilection for the **upper** lid, where the glands are more numerous — the lid the stem names; and it spreads **pagetoid** through the conjunctival epithelium, which is why the diagnosis is made by a pathologist and not by inspection. *(These features are not taken from the course material — the book's ch.5 names no eyelid malignancy at all, and its own audit records that basal cell carcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, sebaceous gland carcinoma and melanoma \"are ALL absent\" from printed 70–81; the book's malignancy chapter is 17, printed 214–217.)* ⚠️ `L5) Eyelid disease` is worse: \"basal\", \"squamous\", \"sebaceous\", \"melanoma\", \"carcinoma\", \"malign\" and \"benign\" **all return zero** across its 170 typed words, and every lid tumour it shows is in its ten silent slides 37–46, which carry no text at all.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Antibiotics and steroids** | Treats a chalazion as an inflammatory lesion — which for a *first* one is reasonable (the book allows intralesional steroid, printed 73, and `opqb-t5-213` keys it). Here it is the specific mistake the rule exists to prevent: it will shrink a carcinoma temporarily and delay the diagnosis by months |\n| **Systemic antibiotics and anti-histamines** | A chalazion is **sterile** and not allergic. Antibiotics belong to the **infected** lesion of `opqb-t5-215` and `opqb-t5-218`; antihistamines have no role at all |\n| **Excision and curette operation** | ⚠️ The right operation for the **wrong** patient, and the sharpest distractor in the topic. Incision and curettage is the standard chalazion procedure (book printed 73; `opqb-t5-193` keys it). But curettage **scoops out contents and does not deliver a specimen** — the very act that has already been done three times without a diagnosis. What is needed is **excision of the lesion with the tissue sent to histopathology** |\n\n**The distinction, in one line.** *Curette* = empty the gland. *Excise and send* = find out what it is. The number of recurrences is what moves the patient from the first to the second.\n\n**Note on the printing.** Option B ends with a comma for a full stop in the source: \"Systemic antibiotics and anti-histamines,\".\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Any chalazion that recurs in the **same site**, in a patient over about 40, or with **lash loss, margin destruction or a yellow tinge**, goes to the laboratory. `opqb-t5-193` uses the opposite error — repeated intralesional steroid for repeated recurrences — as its distractor.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A chalazion recurring in the same site must be excised and sent for histopathology to exclude meibomian gland carcinoma; curettage alone yields no specimen.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.31 (Q222), key p.32' },

  { id:'opqb-t5-223', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-lid',
    stem:'A patient complaining of **foreign body sensation** was found to have **10 mal-directed lashes in the outer third of his upper eye lid**. The **lid margin was otherwise normal**. The best treatment is:',
    options:["Snellen's operation.",'Cryoapplication','Epilation','None of the above'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Two clauses in the stem settle it: the lid margin is *normal*, so this is trichiasis and not entropion; and the lashes are *ten and segmental*, so the treatment must destroy a run of follicles at once.**\n\n**Why trichiasis and not entropion.** The book's discriminator, printed 78: \"The eyelid margin is **in its normal anatomic condition**, but the eyelashes are misdirected toward the eye, causnig [sic] irritation\" — against entropion's \"The eyelid margin is **rotated inwards**\" on printed 77. The stem quotes the discriminator back almost verbatim, and `opqb-t5-208` keys it directly. The symptom fits too: \"The patient complains of chronic red eye, **foreign body sensation**, excessive tearing (reflex lacrimation)\" (printed 78).\n\n**Why cryotherapy and not the other three:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Cryoapplication** | ✅ The book's item 3 (printed 78) and item 3 of the bank's own printed box at `opqb-t5-179`. A **freeze–thaw cycle** destroys lash follicles selectively — they are more cold-sensitive than the surrounding lid tissue — and it treats a **whole segment in one application**, which is what ten contiguous lashes in the outer third need. `L5) Eyelid disease` never mentions it (\"cryo\" returns zero in its typed text), but the book's figure plate on printed 78 photographs \"a cryoprobe applied to an everted lid margin, ice ball visible\" |\n| **Epilation** | ❌ Immediate but **temporary** — the book's own word, printed 78: \"Repeated epilation of the offending lashes. (**Temporary** → Tutorial )\". Follicles are intact, so all ten lashes return in about four to six weeks. It is the right answer to `opqb-t5-221`, which asks for the *holding* measure; it is the wrong answer here, where the stem asks for the **best treatment** |\n| **Snellen's operation** | ❌ A lid-repositioning operation, and the margin here is **normal** — there is nothing to reposition. ⚠️ The eponym appears **nowhere** in the course material: grepped across every file in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and `content\\ophtho\\book\\`, every `Snellen` hit is the **visual-acuity chart**. What the bank's own keys establish is only that it treats Snellen's operation as belonging to **cicatricial entropion of the upper lid** (`opqb-t5-176`, `opqb-t5-197`) |\n| **None of the above** | ❌ Fails because B is correct |\n\n**Why electrolysis is not offered, and it is worth noticing.** Electrolysis — book item 2, \"electrolysis of the lash follicles\" — is definitive and precise, but it treats **one follicle at a time**. For ten lashes it is a long session with a high recurrence rate; for one or two it is ideal. The **number of lashes** is what chooses between the two definitive modalities, and the bank tests exactly that pairing across `opqb-t5-221` and this question. *(The number-based rule is not taken from the course material — the book prints no counts.)*\n\n**The fourth rung, for completeness.** Book item 4: \"surgery. (**mm graft in gray line** → Tutorial)\" — split the lid margin along the **grey line**, cut a groove, interpose a **mucous-membrane graft**, suture it, and the lash line rotates forward away from the globe. ⚠️ The cache flags that this operation \"exists only in these two hand-drawn panels\" on printed 78: the body text gives the bare word \"surgery.\"\n\n**Clinical bottom line — the ladder for trichiasis, by number of lashes.** **One or two → electrolysis. A segment → cryotherapy. The whole margin, or failure of the above → grey-line graft surgery. Any of them, acutely → epilate first.**\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Segmental trichiasis with a normal lid margin is best treated by cryotherapy, which destroys a run of lash follicles in one application; epilation is temporary and lid-repositioning surgery is for entropion.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.31 (Q223), key p.32' }

